00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.400
Okay, let's talk about
the gut and the brain

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.770
and how your gut and your brain

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:02.160
communicate in both directions.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.640
Because as I mentioned before,

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.230
your gut is communicating all the time

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:09.490
with your brain and your brain
is communicating all the time

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:10.400
with your gut.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.080
And so the two are in this
ongoing dance with one another,

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.230
that ordinarily is below
your conscious detection

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.370
although you're probably
familiar with the experience

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:21.740
of every once in a while
getting a stomach ache

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.800
or of eating something that
doesn't agree with you,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.470
or conversely eating something

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.870
that you find particularly delicious

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.830
and that sensation or
that experience rather

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.800
being a whole body experience.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.770
Your mind is excited about
what you're eating or just ate,

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.200
your gut is excited about what
you're eating or just ate,

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.070
and it seems to be a kind
of unified perception

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:44.130
of both brain and body.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.310
Today, we're going to talk
about how that comes about

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:47.690
in the negative sense.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.550
Like, you know, when you meet
someone you really dislike

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:51.440
or when you have a stomach ache,

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:52.910
and in the positive sense,

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:53.850
when you interact with somebody

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:54.980
that you really, really like

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.180
and you'd like to spend more
time with them, for instance,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.470
or when you eat something

00:07:58.470 --> 00:07:59.930
that you really, really like

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:01.850
and you'd like to spend
more time with that food,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.100
so to speak.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:06.150
Now, the gut and the brain
represent what we call

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.230
a biological circuit,

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:10.910
meaning they include different stations.

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:13.640
So station A communicates with station B,

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:16.220
which communicates with
station C, and so on.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:17.920
And as I mentioned earlier,
it is bidirectional.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:21.290
It's a two-way street
between gut and brain.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.040
I want to make the important
point at the outset

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:25.970
that when I say the word gut,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.450
when I refer to the gut,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.520
I am not just referring to the stomach.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.760
Most of us think that the
gut equates to the stomach

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:37.000
because we think of having
a gut or not having a gut

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.790
or having a gut feeling of some sort.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:43.250
But in the context of gut-brain signaling

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.810
and the related microbiome,

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:49.540
the gut includes the
entire digestive tract.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:51.300
That's right, from start to finish

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.080
the entire digestive tract

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.770
so much so that today
we're going to talk about,

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.840
for instance, the presence
of neurons, nerve cells,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.450
that reside in your gut

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.670
that communicate to specific
locations in the brain

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.250
and cause the release of
specific neurochemicals,

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.520
such as the neurochemical
dopamine or serotonin,

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.810
that can motivate you to seek
more of a particular food

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.010
or type of interaction or behavior,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:18.560
or to avoid particular foods,
interactions, and behaviors.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.620
And some of those neurons,
many of those neurons, in fact,

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.710
reside in your intestines,
not in your stomach.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:27.580
They can be in the small
intestine or the large intestine.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:31.160
In fact, you actually have
taste receptors and neurons

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.820
located all along your digestive tract.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.260
You have neurons that are located

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:36.810
all along your digestive tract

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.450
and they are communicating to your brain

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.830
to impact what you think, what
you feel, and what you do.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.340
Okay, so for the gut-brain axis,

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.940
we need to deal with the brain part

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:48.080
and then we need to
deal with the gut part.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:49.870
Let's just quickly talk
about the brain part

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.610
because there the word brain
is also a bit of a misnomer

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.940
in that when we say the gut-brain axis,

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.220
it does include the brain

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:00.310
but includes a lot of
other things as well.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:02.700
So as many of you probably know by now,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.160
if you're listeners of this podcast,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.080
and if you don't, that's fine,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.140
your nervous system includes your brain

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.390
and your spinal cord

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.690
and those together constitute

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:14.280
what's called the central nervous system.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.640
Your neural retinas,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.490
which are lining the back of your eyes

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:22.390
and are the light sensing
portion of your eyes

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:25.720
are also part of your
central nervous system.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.323
So actually your eyes
are part of your brain.

00:10:27.323 --> 00:10:28.970
They're the only parts of your brain

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:31.010
that are outside the cranial vault.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.080
So your retinas, your brain proper,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.620
and your spinal cord make up
the central nervous system.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:38.440
The other parts of your
nervous system constitute

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.990
what's called the
peripheral nervous system,

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:42.690
which are the components
of your nervous system

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:46.650
that reside outside the
retinas, brain, and spinal cord.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:47.760
Now, this is very important

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.380
because today we're going to talk a lot

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.140
about how the gut
communicates with the brain.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.730
And it does that by way

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:57.020
of peripheral nervous system components,

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.500
meaning nerve cells that reside in the gut

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.260
and elsewhere in the body
that communicate to the brain

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.960
and cross into the central nervous system

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.810
to influence what you think
and what you feel, okay?

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.320
So that's the nervous
system part of what we call

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.400
the gut-brain axis,

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:14.870
brain, again, just being a shorthand

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:17.850
for including all the
elements I just described.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:19.070
Gut, as you now know,

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.560
includes all the elements
of the digestive tract.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:24.850
Let's talk about the
architecture or the structure

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.560
of the gut of your digestive system.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.440
Now, not surprisingly
your digestive system,

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:34.150
aka your gut, begins at your
mouth and ends at your anus.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.780
And all along its length,

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.000
there are a series of sphincters

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.340
that cut off certain chambers
of the digestive tract

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.500
from the other chambers.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:47.950
Now, also along this tube that
we call the digestive tract,

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:51.230
there's great variation
in the degree of acidity,

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:53.433
or pH as it's sometimes called.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:58.000
That variation in acidity
turns out to give rise

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.600
to different little microenvironments

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:04.320
in which particular
microbiota, microbacteria,

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.660
can thrive or fail to thrive.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.010
And so the way I'd like you to think

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.330
about the digestive tract,

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:13.420
this gut component of the gut-brain axis,

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.660
is that it's not just one component.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:18.470
It's not just your stomach
with a particular acidity

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:20.360
and a bunch of microorganisms

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.440
that work particularly well

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.590
to make you feel good

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:27.110
and make your digestive
pathways work well.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.380
It's a series of chambers,
little microenvironments,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.320
in which particular microbiota thrive

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.820
and other microbiota do not.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.640
And certain behaviors that you undertake

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.650
and certain experiences that you have

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.630
will adjust those
microenvironments in ways

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.640
that make particular
microbiota, certain bacteria,

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:50.050
more likely to thrive and
others less likely to thrive.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:53.230
We'll talk about how that was
set up for you early in life.

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:55.370
Actually from the moment
that you came into the world,

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:57.730
that microbiome was being established.

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.720
It was actually strongly impacted

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.880
depending on whether or not
you were born by C-section

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.000
or by vaginal birth.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.950
And it was strongly
impacted by who handled you

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:08.900
when you came into the world,

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:10.830
literally the hands that were on you.

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.600
How much skin contact you had,

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.890
whether or not you were
a preemie baby or not,

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.210
whether or not you had pets at home,

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:17.860
whether or not you were
allowed to play in the dirt,

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.280
whether or not you were
allowed to eat snails

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:20.510
or whether or not you were kept

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.420
in a very antiseptic environment.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:23.810
All of those experiences

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:26.220
shaped these little microenvironments

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.030
and shaped what constitutes best or worst

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.910
for those microenvironments, okay?

00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:34.280
So you have this long tube that
we call the digestive tract

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.400
and it's very, very long.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:38.630
In fact, if we were to splay it out,

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:40.880
we were to take all the curves and turns

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.940
out of the intestine, we would
find that it is very long.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:46.940
It's approximately nine meters long.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.710
Now, the structure of that digestive tract

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.300
turns out to be very important

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.313
in terms of gut-brain signaling.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.110
Once again, it's a tube
and the hollow of that tube

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:59.220
is called the lumen, L-U-M-E-N.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.470
But the walls of the tube
are not necessarily smooth,

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.480
at least not for significant portions

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.140
of the digestive tract.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:09.040
For much of the digestive tract,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:11.830
there are bumps and
grooves that look very much

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:13.150
like the folds in the brain,

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:16.010
but these bumps and grooves
are made up of other tissues.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:17.980
They're made up of what's
called a mucosal lining,

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.120
so there's a lot of mucus there.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.400
And if we were to look really closely,

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:23.600
what we'd find is that there are

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:27.170
little hairy-like cellular processes

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.070
that we call microvilli

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.633
that are able to push things
along the digestive tract.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:38.150
The microbiota reside
everywhere along the lumen

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:39.473
of the digestive tract,

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:43.040
starting at the mouth and
all the way to the other end.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.870
And they reside within those microvilli

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:48.130
and they reside within the lumen.

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:51.140
And if we were to look really closely

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:54.330
at the bumps and grooves
along the digestive tract,

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.000
what we would find is that
there are little niches,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.910
little areas in which particular things

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.440
can grow and reside best.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:03.680
Now, that might sound kind of gross,

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.370
but it actually is a good thing,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:08.080
especially what's growing
and residing there

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.530
are microbacterial organisms
that are good for your gut

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:14.300
and that signal good things to your brain.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.440
And we will talk about what
that signaling looks like

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.250
and how that's done and
accomplished in just a few moments.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:22.320
But I want you to get a clear
mental picture of your gut,

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:23.990
something that we don't often see.

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:25.580
And often when we think about the gut,

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.810
again, we just think about
the hollow of the stomach,

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.820
food going in there, and getting digested,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.150
but it's far more complex

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:33.170
and actually far more
interesting than that.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:35.380
Now, I've been referring
to the gut microbiome

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:38.320
and to the microbiota and these bacteria.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.820
Let me define those terms a
little bit more specifically

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:42.663
just to avoid any confusion.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:46.940
The microbiota are the actual bacteria.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:50.170
The microbiome is used
to refer to the bacteria

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:52.830
but also all the genes as bacteria make,

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.710
because it turns out that
they make some important genes

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:56.603
that actually impact all of us.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:00.240
You have loads and loads

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:03.410
of these little
microbiota, these bacteria.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.060
In fact, right now you
are carrying with you

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:08.220
about two to three kilograms,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.340
so that's more than six pounds,

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:13.100
of these microbiota, these bacteria.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.900
And if we were to look at
them under a microscope,

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:15.733
what we would see

00:16:15.733 --> 00:16:18.510
is these are relatively
simple little organisms.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:21.470
Some remain stationary
so they might plop down

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:22.940
into the mucosal lining

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.450
or they might hang out on
a particular microvilli

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:28.170
or they might be in one
of those little niches,

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.150
and others can move about,

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:33.010
but they basically fill the entire lumen.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:36.040
They surround and kind of coat the surface

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:38.570
of the microvilli and they're tucked up

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:42.210
into any of those little niches
that are available to them

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:43.223
to tuck into.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:46.850
If you were to take the head of a pin

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.210
and look at it under the microscope,

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:50.950
you could fit many, many hundreds,

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:55.510
if not thousands or more of
these little microbacteria.

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.450
And the reason I say many,
many thousands or more,

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:00.290
I'm giving a kind of broad range there,

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:02.130
is that they do vary in size

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:03.870
and, again, they vary as to
whether or not they can move

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.620
or they don't move.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:08.070
Now, they're constantly
turning over in your gut,

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:10.520
meaning they're being born so to speak

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.670
and they're dying off.

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:14.410
And some will stay there

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.330
for very long periods
of time within your gut

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:18.130
and others will get excreted.

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:20.950
About 60% of your stool,

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.520
as unpleasant as that
might be to think about,

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:27.730
is made up of live and dead microbacteria.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.220
So you're constantly making

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.250
and excreting these microbacteria.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.340
And which microbacteria you make

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.700
and how many stay inside
your gut and how many leave,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.203
meaning how many are excreted,

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:45.330
depends a lot on the chemistry of your gut

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.760
and depends very strongly
on the foods that you eat

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.940
and the foods that you do not eat.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.480
Now, just because what we
eat strongly influences

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.970
our microbiome, meaning our microbacteria,

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:00.510
does not mean that there
are not other influences

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:02.893
on what constitutes our microbiome.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:07.480
Our microbiome is also
made up by microbacteria

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:10.020
that access our digestive
tract through our mouth,

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:13.830
through breathing, through kissing,

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:16.120
and through skin contact.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:19.700
In fact, one of the major
determinants of our microbiome

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:21.670
is who we interact with

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:24.060
and the environment
that we happen to be in.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.570
And that actually includes whether or not

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.850
we interact with animals.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:30.200
In a little bit, I'll talk about some data

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.350
as to whether or not you grew
up in a home that had animals,

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:34.520
whether or not you grew up in the home,

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:36.410
whether or not there was
a lot of social contact,

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:38.070
meaning skin contact,

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:39.420
or whether or not you grew up

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:43.610
in a more animal sparse,
contact sparse environment

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:45.810
and how that shapes your microbiome.

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:48.370
But the simple point is that what you eat

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.100
influences your microbiome,
but also what you do,

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:52.400
what you think, and what you feel,

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:55.140
and many of the low microbacteria

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:57.440
that get into your digestive tract

00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:59.960
do so by way of social interactions.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.550
In fact, if you ask a neurobiologist

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:04.660
what the role of the microbiome is,

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:06.990
they'll tell you almost certainly

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.260
that it's there to impact brain function.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:14.460
But if you have friends
that are microbiologists,

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.660
such as I do, they'll tell you,

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.760
well, maybe the brain and
nervous system are there

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.040
to support the microbiome.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:22.600
It's the other way around.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.110
You have all these little microorganisms

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:26.800
that are taking residence in our body.

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:29.480
They don't really know what
they're doing as far as we know.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.450
We don't know that they have
consciousness or they don't.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.840
We can't rule that out, but
it seems pretty unlikely.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:36.360
Nonetheless, they are taking advantage

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.260
of the different environments

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:39.800
all along your digestive tract.

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:41.620
They are taking advantage

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:43.830
of the sorts of social interactions,

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:44.957
for instance, the people you talk to

00:19:44.957 --> 00:19:46.260
and that breathe on you,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.010
the people that you shake hands with,

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.140
the people that you kiss or don't kiss,

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:51.150
the people that you happen to be

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:52.890
romantically involved with or not,

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:54.930
your dog, your cat, your lizard, your rat,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:59.120
whatever pet you happen to own
is impacting your microbiome.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:01.100
There's absolutely no question about that.

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.520
So hopefully now you have
some sense of the architecture

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.360
of the digestive pathway

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.260
and you have some sense of the trillions

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.490
of little microbacteria that are living

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:12.270
all along the different components

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.350
of that digestive pathway.

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:15.950
But what we haven't talked about yet,

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:17.630
and what I'd like to talk about now,

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:21.280
is what those little
microbiota are actually doing

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.150
in your digestive tract.

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:24.340
In addition to just living there

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:26.350
for their own intents and purposes,

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:29.510
they are contributing, for
instance, to your digestion.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:32.860
Many of the genes that
those microbiota make

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:35.330
are genes that are
involved in fermentation

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.600
and genes that are involved in digestion

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.050
of particular types of nutrients.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:41.680
And in a little bit, we will talk

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:46.160
about how what you eat can
actually change the enzymes

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:49.150
that those microbiome components make,

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:50.760
enzymes largely being things

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.750
that are responsible for digestion.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:54.830
They catalyze other
sorts of cellular events

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:56.910
but in the context of
the digestive pathway,

00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.330
we're talking about enzymes
that help digest your food.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:02.800
So those microbiota are indeed
helping you in many ways.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:04.460
And if you lack certain microbiota

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:06.140
that can help you digest,

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:07.930
it stands to reason that
you would have challenges

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:09.980
digesting certain types of foods.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:13.540
The other amazing thing
that these microbiota do

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:16.730
is they change the way
that your brain functions

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:20.050
by way of metabolizing or facilitating

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:23.230
the metabolism of particular
neurotransmitters.

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:25.900
So one of the ways that having
certain microbiota present

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:29.820
in your gut can improve your
mood or degrade your mood,

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:34.560
for instance, is by way
of certain microbiota

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.170
being converted into or
facilitating the conversion

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:41.020
of chemicals, such as GABA.

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:44.550
GABA is what we call an
inhibitory neurotransmitter.

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:47.530
It's involved in suppressing the action

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:48.900
of other neurons.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:50.810
And that might sound like a bad thing,

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.110
but all types of sedatives, for instance,

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:57.890
alcohol, and a lot of neurons
that naturally make GABA

00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:59.780
can help quiet certain
circuits in the brain,

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:03.280
for instance, circuits
responsible for anxiety.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.280
In people who have epilepsy,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:07.810
the GABAergic neurons, as they're called,

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.560
can all often be disrupted
in their signaling,

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.620
meaning they're not
cranking out as much GABA

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:15.170
and therefore the excitatory neurons,

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:17.920
which typically release other
molecules like glutamate

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.980
can engage in what's
called runaway excitation

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.020
and that can give rise to seizures.

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:26.460
So the simple message here
is that the microbiota

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.870
by way of making neurochemicals

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:31.350
can influence the way
that your brain functions.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.040
So you want to support those microbiota

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:36.830
and we will give you tools
to support those microbiota.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:39.310
But the takeaway at this point

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:41.990
is that those microbiota
are making things locally

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:43.470
to help digest food.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.080
Other microbiota are helping to make

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:48.040
certain neurotransmitters like GABA,

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:50.590
and we'll also talk about
dopamine and serotonin.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:54.860
And so the very specific
microbiota that reside in your gut

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:56.290
have a profound influence

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.710
on many, many biological functions,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:01.100
especially immune system function,

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:03.690
brain function, and digestion.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.900
So that should give you
a fairly complete picture

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:08.300
of your gut microbiome.

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:10.720
Now I'd like to talk
about how your microbiome

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:12.460
and your brain communicate,

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.330
or more accurately, how your microbiome

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:16.920
and the rest of your
nervous system communicate.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:19.650
Neurons, which simply means nerve cells,

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:22.370
are the cells that do
most of the heavy lifting

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:23.320
in your nervous system.

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.410
There are of course other
cell types that are important.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.380
Glial cell, for instance, very,
very important cell types.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.740
You have endothelial cells,

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:31.470
which are responsible for blood flow,

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.750
pericytes and other types of cells,

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:36.680
but the neurons are really
doing most of the heavy lifting

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:38.440
for most of the things we think about

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.343
in terms of nervous system function.

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:44.210
You have neurons in your gut

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:46.133
and that should not surprise you.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.940
Neurons reside in your brain,
your spinal cord, your eyes,

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:51.760
in fact, all over your body,

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:54.070
and you've got them on your
heart and in your heart,

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:56.360
and you've got them in your lungs,

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.260
and you've got them in your spleen,

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:00.660
and they connect to all
the different organs

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:02.820
and tissues of your body.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:05.530
So that's not surprising that
you have neurons in your gut.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.240
What is surprising, however,

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:09.970
is the presence of
particular types of neurons

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:13.920
that reside near or in the mucosal lining

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.780
just next to that lumen of the gut

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.910
and that are paying attention,

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:20.830
and I'll explain what I
mean by paying attention,

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:24.095
to the components of the
gut, both the nutrients

00:24:24.095 --> 00:24:26.740
and the microbiota,

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:29.840
and thereby can send signals

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:33.470
up to the brain by way of a long wire

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:37.900
that we call an axon, and can communicate

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:42.160
what the chemistry and what
the nutritional quality

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:44.940
and what the other aspects of
the environment are at the gut

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.670
at a given location up to the brain

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.950
in ways that can influence
the brain to, for instance,

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:52.640
seek out more of a particular food.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:55.500
Let me give you a sort of
action-based picture of this.

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:59.260
Let's say like most people,
you enjoy sweet foods.

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:00.880
I don't particularly enjoy sweet foods

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:01.950
but there are a few that I like.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:05.500
I'm a sucker for a really
good dark chocolate

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:07.360
or really good ice cream

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:09.330
or I got this thing for donuts

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:10.860
that seems to just not quit

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:13.360
although I don't tend to
indulge it very often,

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.193
I do like them.

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:17.010
If I eat that particular food,

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:19.470
obviously digestion starts in the mouth.

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.070
There are enzymes there,
it gets chewed up,

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:22.670
the food goes down into the gut.

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:27.290
These neurons are activated,

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:30.830
meaning that causes the neurons
to be electrically active

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:32.480
when particular components,

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:35.390
certain nutrients in
those foods are present.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:37.300
And for the cell types,

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:40.580
or I should say the neuron
types that matter here,

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:43.470
the nutrients that really
trigger their activation

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:46.060
are sugar, fatty acids,

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:47.293
and amino acids.

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:50.330
Now, these particular neurons

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:53.210
have the name enteroendocrine cells

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:57.460
but more recently they've been
defined as neuropod cells.

00:25:57.460 --> 00:25:58.630
Neuropod cells were discovered

00:25:58.630 --> 00:26:02.280
by Diego Bohorquez's
lab at Duke University.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:03.890
This is a phenomenal set of discoveries

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.370
made mostly in the last 10 years.

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:08.760
These neuropod cells, as I mentioned,

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:12.570
are activated by sugar,
fatty acids, or amino acids,

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:17.430
but have a particularly
strong activation to sugars.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:20.430
They do seem to be part of
the sweet sensing system.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:22.970
And even though I'm focusing
on this particular example,

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.720
they represent a really nice example

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:29.410
of how a particular set
of neuro cells in our gut

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.900
is collecting information
about what is there

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:33.460
at a particular location in the gut,

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:36.050
and sending that
information up to our brain.

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:39.260
Now, they do that by
way of a nerve pathway

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:40.950
called the vagus nerve.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:43.240
The vagus nerve is part of
the peripheral nervous system.

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:46.420
And the vagus nerve is a
little bit complex to describe

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.270
if you're just listening to this.

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:49.490
If you are watching this,

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:51.610
I'll try and use my hands as a diagram

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.340
but really the best thing to do

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.650
if you want really want
to learn neuroanatomy

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:58.080
is to just imagine it in
your mind as best you can

00:26:58.080 --> 00:26:59.890
and if you can track down
a picture of it, terrific,

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:01.840
but here's how it works.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:04.130
Neurons have a cell body
that we call a soma,

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:07.450
that's where all the DNA are contained.

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:09.770
That's where a lot of
the operating machinery

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:11.330
of the cells are contained

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.340
and a lot of the
instructions for that cell

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:16.360
of what to be and how to
operate are contained.

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:20.100
The cell bodies of these
neurons or the relevant neurons

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:22.090
are actually up near the neck.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:25.330
So you can think of them
as a clump of grapes,

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:28.370
'cause cell bodies tend
to be round or oval-ish.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:32.840
And then they send a
process that we call an axon

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.460
in one direction out to the gut

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:40.540
and they'll send another
process up into the brain.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:42.580
And that little cluster near the neck

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:43.907
that's relevant here is called

00:27:43.907 --> 00:27:47.380
the nodose ganglion, N-O-D-O-S-E.

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:50.180
The nodose ganglion is a
little cluster of neurons

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:51.890
on either side of the neck.

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:53.267
It has a process that goes out to the gut

00:27:53.267 --> 00:27:55.387
and a process that goes up into the brain.

00:27:55.387 --> 00:27:57.840
And again, these are just one component

00:27:57.840 --> 00:27:59.383
of the so-called vagus nerve.

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:02.780
The vagus nerve has many, many branches,

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:03.850
not just to the gut.

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:06.420
There are also branches to the liver,

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:07.470
branches to the lungs,

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:10.470
branches to the heart,
branches to the larynx,

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:11.990
and even to the spleen,

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.900
and other areas of the
body that are important.

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:16.740
But right now we're just
concentrating on the neurons

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.240
that are in the gut that
signal up to the brain.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:22.590
And what the Bohorquez lab has shown

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.760
is that these neuropod
cells are part of network.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:28.320
They're sensing several
different nutrients,

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:30.330
but in particular, when they sense sugar,

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:34.200
they send signals in the
form of electrical firing

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:37.130
up to the brain in ways
that trigger activation

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:40.940
of other brain stations that
cause you to seek out more

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:42.333
of that particular food.

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:45.620
Now, this brings us to
some classic experiments

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:48.440
that at least to me are incredible.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:50.770
And these are highly reproducible findings

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:52.200
showing, for instance,

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:54.940
that even if you bypass taste

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:57.960
by infusing sweet liquid

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:01.740
or putting sweet foods into the gut,

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:04.510
and people can never taste
them with their mouth,

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:07.810
people will seek out more
of that particular food.

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.320
And if you give them the option

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:13.920
to have a sweet food
infused into their gut

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:16.870
or a bitter food infused into their gut

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:19.170
or a sweet versus sour,

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:21.800
or a more sweet versus less sweet food,

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:25.210
people have a selective
preference for sweet foods

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:27.330
even if they can't taste them.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:28.910
Now, this is important to understand

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.450
in the context of gut-brain signaling

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:33.080
because we always think
that we like sweet foods

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.800
because of the way they taste.

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:37.150
And indeed that's still true,

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:41.490
but much of what we consider
the great taste of a sweet food

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.770
also has to do with a gut sensation

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:46.380
that is below our conscious detection.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:47.520
How do we know that?

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:51.040
Well, the Bohorquez lab
has performed experiments

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:53.330
using modern methods and
they're classic experiments

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.340
showing that animals and humans

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:58.960
will actively seek out more
of a particular sweet food

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:01.790
even if it bypasses this taste system.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:04.060
And the reverse is also true.

00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:07.280
There have been experiments
done in animals and in humans

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:09.550
that have allowed animals or humans

00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:11.780
to select and eat sweet foods,

00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:14.730
and indeed that's what they do
if they're given the option.

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:20.730
And yet to somehow eliminate
the activation of these neurons

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:23.700
within the gut that can sense sweet foods.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:24.750
Now, there are a couple different ways

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:26.240
that those experiments ave been done.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:29.470
In classic experiments
that date back to the '80s,

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:32.520
this was done by what's called
subdiaphragmatic vagotomy.

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:34.690
So this means cutting off
the branch of the vagus

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:37.200
that enervates the gut below the diaphragm

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:38.960
so that the other organs
can still function

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:40.650
because the vagus is very important,

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:43.460
but basically cutting off
the sweet sensing in the gut,

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:45.750
still giving people the
opportunity to taste sweet foods

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:46.583
with their mouth,

00:30:46.583 --> 00:30:48.490
and they don't actively seek out

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.530
quite as much of the sweet food

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:52.830
when they don't have this
gut sensing mechanism

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:55.973
that we now know to be dependent
on these neuropod cells.

00:30:57.060 --> 00:30:59.600
More recent experiments
involve selective silencing

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:01.170
of these neuropod cells.

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:02.950
And there have been a lot
of different derivations

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:03.940
of this sort of thing,

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:07.040
but the takeaway from it
is that our experience of

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.070
and our desire for particular foods

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:11.500
has everything to do with
how those foods taste.

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:13.840
It also has to do, as you probably know,

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:17.670
with their texture and the
sensation of those foods

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:20.690
in our mouth, and even indeed
how they go down our throat

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:23.190
sometimes can be very
pleasing or very unpleasant.

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:28.220
And it also has to do with
this subconscious processing

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:31.920
of taste that occurs in the gut itself.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.120
And again, when I say gut, I
don't just mean in the stomach.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.310
There are actually
neurons, neuropod cells,

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:39.070
further down your digestive tract

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:40.910
which are signaling to your brain

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:42.440
about the presence of sweet foods,

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:46.110
as well as foods such
as amino acid rich foods

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:49.430
or foods that are rich in
particular types of fatty acids

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:50.840
signaling up to your brain

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:54.850
and causing you to seek
out more of those foods

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:57.200
or to consume more of those foods.

00:31:57.200 --> 00:31:59.330
Now, you're probably
asking, what is the signal?

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:01.930
How does it actually make
me want more of those foods

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:03.330
without me realizing it?

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:05.390
Well, it does that by
adjusting the release

00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:07.840
of particular neuromodulators.

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:10.230
For those of you that are not
familiar with neuromodulators,

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.790
these are similar to neurotransmitters,

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:13.800
but they tend to act more broadly.

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:16.270
They tend to impact many
more neurons all at once

00:32:16.270 --> 00:32:19.030
and they go by names
like dopamine, serotonin,

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:21.670
acetylcholine, epinephrine, and so forth.

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:24.180
Sometimes people refer to
those as neurotransmitters.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.890
Technically they are neuromodulators.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:28.870
I'll refer to them almost
always as neuromodulators.

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:31.530
The neuropod cells signal by way

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:33.920
of a particular branch of the vagus

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:36.990
through that nodose ganglion
that we talked about before

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:39.770
and through a number of different stations

00:32:39.770 --> 00:32:40.880
in the brain stem,

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:45.043
eventually cause the release
of the neuromodulator dopamine.

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:48.040
Dopamine is often associated

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:49.810
with a sense of pleasure and reward

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:53.250
but it is more appropriately
thought of as a neuromodulator

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:55.850
that impacts motivation,
craving, and pursuit.

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:57.870
It tends to put us into modes of action,

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:00.620
not necessarily running
and moving through space,

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:03.390
although it can do that too,
but in the context of feeding,

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:07.020
it tends to make us look around more,

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.140
chew more, reach for things more,

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:10.750
and seek out more of whatever it is

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:13.070
that's giving us that sensation

00:33:13.070 --> 00:33:15.750
of delight or satisfaction.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.640
And again, that sense of
delight and satisfaction,

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:21.000
you might experience only consciously

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:23.310
as the way that something
tastes on your mouth

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:25.240
but it actually is caused again

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:27.850
by both the sensations in your mouth

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:31.120
but also by the activation
of these neuropod cells.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:34.150
So this is an incredible
system of gut-brain signaling,

00:33:34.150 --> 00:33:36.740
and it is but one system
of gut-brain signaling.

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:39.780
It turns out it's the system
that we know the most about

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:41.170
at this point in time.

00:33:41.170 --> 00:33:43.190
There are other components
of gut-brain signaling

00:33:43.190 --> 00:33:44.840
that we'll talk about in
a moment, for instance,

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:46.900
the serotonin system.

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:49.470
But in terms of examples
of gut-brain signaling

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.850
for which we know a lot
of the individual elements

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:52.870
and how they work,

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:57.330
I think this neuropod, neuron
sensing of sweet foods,

00:33:57.330 --> 00:33:59.470
fatty acids, and amino acids in the gut

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:02.360
and communicating that up to the brain

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:04.110
by way of the vagus and causing us

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:05.430
to seek out more of the foods

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:08.890
that deliver those nutrients
is an incredible pathway

00:34:08.890 --> 00:34:11.380
that really delineates
the beauty and the power

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:13.290
of this gut-brain axis.

00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:14.570
Let me talk about timescales.

00:34:14.570 --> 00:34:17.280
Here I'm talking about a
particular type of neuron

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:20.940
that is signaling up to the
brain using electrical signals

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:23.080
to cause us to want to seek out

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:25.363
a particular category of foods.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:28.800
That's happening relatively fast

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:31.110
compared to the hormone
pathways of the gut

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:32.640
which also involve neurons.

00:34:32.640 --> 00:34:35.130
So your gut is also
communicating to your brain

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:36.933
by way of neurons, nerve cells.

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:41.960
But some of those nerve
cells also release hormones.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:44.330
And those hormones go by names like CCK,

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:47.823
glucagon-like peptide 1, PYY, et cetera.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.730
A good example of a hormone pathway,

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:54.460
or what's sometimes called
a hormone peptide pathway,

00:34:54.460 --> 00:34:58.210
that is similar to the pathway
I've talked about before

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:01.770
but a little bit slower
is the ghrelin pathway.

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:04.870
Ghrelin, G-H-R-E-L-I-N,

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:07.660
increases with fasting.

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:10.140
So the longer it's been
since you've eaten,

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:12.240
or if you're just eating very little food

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:14.060
compared to your caloric needs,

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:16.400
ghrelin levels are going to
go up in your bloodstream

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:18.770
and they go up because of processes

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:21.180
that include processes within the gut

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:22.940
and include the nervous system.

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:24.870
So it's a slow pathway driving you

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.090
to seek out food generally.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:30.120
As far as we know, the
ghrelin system is not partial

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:33.263
to seeking out of sweet foods
or fatty foods or so on.

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:38.550
Ghrelin increases the longer it's been

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:40.610
since you've eaten sufficient calories

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:44.170
and it stimulates a feeling of
you wanting to seek out food.

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:46.030
Well, how does it do that?

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:48.320
It does that again by
impacting neural circuits

00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:50.720
within the brain, neural
circuits that include

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:52.850
what we call the brain
stem autonomic center.

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:54.910
So it tends to make you feel alert

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:58.520
and quite, we say, high
levels of autonomic arousal.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:35:59.970
If you haven't eaten in a while,

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:02.200
you might think that you just
get really exhausted, right?

00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:03.900
Because we all hear that food is energy

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:05.990
and caloric energy is
what we need to burn,

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.400
but you actually have a lot
of energy stored in your body

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:10.660
that you would be able to use

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:12.850
if you really needed energy.

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:14.380
But typically if we
haven't eaten in a while,

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:15.880
we start to get agitated

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:17.790
and we get agitated by way way of release

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.050
of the neuromodulator epinephrine,

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:22.950
which causes us to look
around more, move around more,

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:24.123
and seek out food.

00:36:25.130 --> 00:36:27.540
That all occurs in brain
stem autonomic centers

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:28.580
and in the hypothalamus.

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:30.960
We did an entire episode
on feeding behavior

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:32.670
and metabolism as well

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:34.790
and you can find those
episodes at hubermanlab.com

00:36:34.790 --> 00:36:36.470
so I don't want to go into a lot of detail

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:38.710
about hypothalamic and
brains stem centers.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:40.800
But there's a particular area of the brain

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:43.270
called the nucleus of the solitary tract,

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:46.740
the NST as it's called,
that's very strongly impacted

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:48.280
by these circulating hormones

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:50.730
and tends to drive us
toward feeding behavior.

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:53.960
So the important point here
is that we have a fast system

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:56.090
that is paying attention
to the nutrients in our gut

00:36:56.090 --> 00:36:57.710
or the absence of nutrients in our gut,

00:36:57.710 --> 00:36:59.600
and stimulating us to seek out food

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:01.350
or to stop eating certain foods,

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:04.710
and we have a slower
hormone-related system

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:07.540
that also originates in the
gut and impacts the brain.

00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:12.360
But all of those converge on
neural circuits for feeding.

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:13.520
The neural circuits for feeding

00:37:13.520 --> 00:37:15.870
include things like the arcuate
nucleus of the hypothalamus,

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:18.050
they include a bunch of
other neurochemicals,

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:20.440
but the point is that
you've got a fast route

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:24.800
and a slow route to drive
you to eat more or eat less,

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:28.790
right, to seek out food and
consume it or to stop eating,

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:32.490
to essentially kickstart
the satiety mechanisms

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:33.740
as they're called,

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:35.830
and those are operating in parallel.

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:37.200
It's not like one happens first

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:38.070
then stops then the other.

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:39.670
They're always operating in parallel.

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:42.060
And I bring this up because
there's a bigger theme here

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:44.280
which we see over and
over again in biology,

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.170
which is the concept of parallel pathways.

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:49.210
You've always got multiple
accelerators and multiple brakes

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:50.043
on a system.

00:37:50.043 --> 00:37:52.200
It's very, very rare to
have just one accelerator

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:53.610
and one brake on the system.

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:54.930
And this will become important later

00:37:54.930 --> 00:37:58.300
when we talk about tools for
optimizing your gut microbiome

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:00.610
for healthy eating and for
health healthy digestion

00:38:00.610 --> 00:38:02.070
and for healthy brain function.

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:03.210
I want to take a moment and talk

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.880
about glucagon-like peptide 1,

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:09.895
which is also called GLP-1.

00:38:09.895 --> 00:38:13.800
GLP-1 is made by neurons in the gut

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.693
and by neurons in the brain.

00:38:15.693 --> 00:38:17.800
This is a fairly recent discovery

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:20.300
but it's an important one.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:24.210
GLP-1 tends to inhibit feeding

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:26.433
and tends to reduce appetite.

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:31.840
There are a number of drugs
released on the market now,

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:34.810
one for instance goes
by the name semaglutide,

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:38.280
which is essentially an GLP-1 agonist.

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:40.420
It causes the release of more GLP-1.

00:38:40.420 --> 00:38:42.600
It's being used to treat type II diabetes,

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:44.420
which is insulin resistant diabetes.

00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:46.360
This is different than type I diabetes

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.790
where people don't actually make insulin.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:53.790
It's also being used as
a drug to reduce obesity.

00:38:54.020 --> 00:38:55.610
And it seems pretty effective

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:57.940
at least in certain populations.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:01.220
There are certain foods and
substances that increase GLP-1.

00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:03.320
I've talked about a few
of these on the podcast.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:06.040
One that I'm a particular fan
of for entirely other reasons

00:39:06.040 --> 00:39:10.620
is yerba mate tea can
stimulate the release of GLP-1.

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:12.010
In South America, it's often used

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:13.280
as an appetite suppressant,

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:14.410
probably in large part

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:16.803
because of its effect on GLP-1 release,

00:39:17.840 --> 00:39:20.400
but probably also because
it does contain caffeine,

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:21.530
which is a bit of a stimulant,

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:23.320
which also can be involved in lipolysis,

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:24.970
which is the utilization of fat stores

00:39:24.970 --> 00:39:26.203
for energy and so forth.

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:29.060
A brief mention about yerba mate.

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:32.690
There are some reports
out there that yerba mate

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:35.280
can increase certain types of cancers.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.870
The data that I've seen on this

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:39.760
is that it tends to
relate to whether or not

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:42.500
those are smoked versions
of the yerba mate tea,

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:44.370
the amount of consumption,

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:45.720
and the debate is still out.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.370
So I invite you to look at those papers.

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:49.053
You can search for those online.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:55.200
Nonetheless, yerba mate is one
source of GLP-1 stimulation.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:56.750
Semaglutide is another source.

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:59.120
It also can be stimulated
by various foods, nuts,

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:01.920
avocados, eggs, and so forth.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.300
Certain high fiber complex grains

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:06.740
will also stimulate GLP-1.

00:40:06.740 --> 00:40:09.940
I raise this as not necessarily a route

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.710
that you want to take in
order to reduce food intake.

00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:14.560
I don't even know that that's your goal.

00:40:14.560 --> 00:40:18.620
But that GLP-1 is another one

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:20.690
of these gut-to-brain signaling mechanisms

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:24.550
that adjusts appetite
that is dependent on diet,

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:26.780
depends on what you eat or drink,

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:28.800
and that the GLP-1 pathway

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:30.650
does seem particularly sensitive

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:32.680
to the constituents of diet.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:34.450
There's at least one quality study

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:35.450
I was able to find showing

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:37.510
that the ketogenic diet for instance,

00:40:37.510 --> 00:40:40.400
which almost always involves ingestion

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:42.270
of very low levels of carbohydrate

00:40:42.270 --> 00:40:44.140
can increase GLP-1.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:45.260
Although, as I mentioned before,

00:40:45.260 --> 00:40:46.490
there are other foods that fall

00:40:46.490 --> 00:40:49.370
outside the range of what
we would consider ketogenic

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:50.920
that can also stimulate GLP-1,

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:51.753
and as I mentioned,

00:40:51.753 --> 00:40:54.010
there are prescription
drugs, like semaglutide,

00:40:54.010 --> 00:40:55.280
there are other ones as well now,

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:57.510
that stimulate GLP-1.

00:40:57.510 --> 00:40:59.500
So how does GLP-1 reduce appetite?

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:02.810
It does that, in part, by
changing the activity of neurons

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:03.643
in the hypothalamus,

00:41:03.643 --> 00:41:06.920
this cluster of neurons just
above the roof of our mouth,

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:09.740
that themselves make GLP-1

00:41:09.740 --> 00:41:12.920
and that cause the
activation of motor circuits

00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:15.563
for reaching, chewing, all the things

00:41:15.563 --> 00:41:18.070
that we associate with feeding behavior.

00:41:18.070 --> 00:41:20.390
So I use GLP-1 as an example of a pathway

00:41:20.390 --> 00:41:22.040
that you might choose to tap into

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:23.500
by ingestion of yerba mate

00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:25.230
or by ingestion of the foods I mentioned,

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:28.090
or if it's something that
interests you, ketogenic diet.

00:41:28.090 --> 00:41:30.110
But I also mention it simply

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:32.660
because it's another beautiful example

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.450
of how a hormone pathway
can impact the activity

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:37.600
of brain circuits that
are directly involved

00:41:37.600 --> 00:41:39.400
in a particular behavior.

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:43.580
So yet another example of how
gut is communicating to brain

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:46.450
in order to change what we think we want

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:49.100
or to change what our
actual behaviors are.

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:51.430
So the next time you find
yourself reaching for food,

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:55.440
or you find yourself wanting
a particular sweet thing

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:58.150
or fatty thing or something that contains

00:41:58.150 --> 00:41:59.550
a lot of amino acids,

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:01.670
a protein rich food,

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:03.230
keep in mind that that's not just

00:42:03.230 --> 00:42:04.620
about the taste of the food,

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:06.690
and it's not even necessarily
about the nutrients

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:08.410
that you need or don't need.

00:42:08.410 --> 00:42:09.243
It could be,

00:42:09.243 --> 00:42:11.500
but it's also about this
subconscious signaling

00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:13.400
that's coming from your body all the time,

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:17.530
waves of hormones, waves
of nerve cell signals,

00:42:17.530 --> 00:42:19.670
electrical signals that
are changing the way

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:21.470
that your brain works.

00:42:21.470 --> 00:42:23.170
And this raises for me

00:42:23.170 --> 00:42:27.840
a memory of the episode that I
did with Dr. Robert Sapolsky,

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:30.540
who's a world expert
colleague of mine at Stanford,

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:34.230
who is expert on things
like hormones and behavior.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:36.370
But we got into the topic of free will,

00:42:36.370 --> 00:42:38.530
which is a bit of a barbed wire topic

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:39.363
as many of you know.

00:42:39.363 --> 00:42:41.147
It gets into the realm
of philosophy, et cetera.

00:42:41.147 --> 00:42:43.340
And we were kind of batting back and forth

00:42:43.340 --> 00:42:46.950
the idea, I was saying, "Well,
I think there's free will,

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:48.780
and can't there certainly be free will

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:53.020
or certainly the idea that we
can avoid certain choices?"

00:42:53.020 --> 00:42:55.940
And Robert was saying, "No."

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:58.760
In fact, he said, "Nah,"
he doesn't believe

00:42:58.760 --> 00:42:59.850
that we have any free will.

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:03.040
He thinks that events in
our brain are determined

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:06.810
by biological events that are
below our conscious detection

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:09.080
and that occur seconds to milliseconds

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:11.280
before we make decisions or assessments

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:14.540
and, therefore, we just
can't control what we do,

00:43:14.540 --> 00:43:16.370
what we think, and what we feel.

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:19.080
And at the time I sort of didn't buy it.

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:20.510
I thought, I don't know.

00:43:20.510 --> 00:43:23.280
I just, I guess I really
wanted to believe in free will.

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:25.040
And to some extent I still do

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:27.377
but as we talk about how
these neurons in our gut

00:43:27.377 --> 00:43:30.650
and these hormones in our
gut are influencing our brain

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:32.340
and the decisions that we are making,

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.690
at the level of circuits,
like the hypothalamus

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:36.560
and the nucleus of the solitary tract,

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:39.670
these are areas of the brain
way below our frontal cortex

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:41.193
and our conscious perception.

00:43:42.050 --> 00:43:43.780
Think these are examples that really fall

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:46.950
in favor of what Dr. Sapolsky was arguing,

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:49.650
which is that events that
are happening within our body

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:51.200
are actually changing
the way our brain works.

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:53.260
So we might think that
we want the cupcake.

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:56.830
We might think that we
don't need to eat something

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.170
or do need to eat something

00:43:58.170 --> 00:44:01.700
and that is entirely on the
basis of prior knowledge

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:04.050
and decision-making that
we're making with our head,

00:44:04.050 --> 00:44:04.940
but in fact,

00:44:04.940 --> 00:44:07.070
it's very clear to me based on the work

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:08.450
from the Bohorquez lab,

00:44:08.450 --> 00:44:11.000
classic work over the years
dating back to the '80s,

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:12.210
and indeed back to the '50s

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:13.720
that we'll talk about in a moment,

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:16.430
that our body is shaping the decisions

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:19.630
that our brain is making and
we're not aware of it at all.

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:21.900
Now, the good news is that
whether or not you believe

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:24.120
in free will or not, the simple knowledge

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:26.340
that this whole process is happening

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:27.930
can perhaps be a benefit to you.

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:31.110
You can perhaps leverage
it to get some insight

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:32.840
and understanding and perhaps even a wedge

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:33.700
into your own behavior.

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:37.180
You might think, ah, I think
I want that particular food,

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:39.540
or I think I want to avoid
that particular food,

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:41.950
but actually that's not a decision

00:44:41.950 --> 00:44:44.200
that I'm making on a
purely rational basis.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:47.030
Has a lot to do with what
my gut is telling my brain.

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:50.160
So we've largely been talking
about chemical communication

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:51.770
between the gut and the brain.

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:55.630
Chemical because even
though these neuropod cells

00:44:55.630 --> 00:44:57.040
are communicating with the brain

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.080
by way of electrical activity,

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:00.660
what we call action potentials,

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:03.400
and in neural language
we call those spikes,

00:45:03.400 --> 00:45:05.013
spikes of action potentials,

00:45:06.580 --> 00:45:09.400
spikes of action potentials,

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:10.630
meaning those neural signals,

00:45:10.630 --> 00:45:14.223
cause the release of chemicals
in the brain like dopamine.

00:45:15.450 --> 00:45:17.003
So it's chemical transmission.

00:45:18.550 --> 00:45:21.410
Similarly, hormones, even
though they act more slowly,

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:26.000
hormones like neuropeptide
Y like CCK, like ghrelin,

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:28.070
they are signaling chemically.

00:45:28.070 --> 00:45:30.140
They're moving through the body,

00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:32.990
they're going in there
affecting the chemical output

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:34.170
of different cells,

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:35.937
and they're changing the
chemistry of those cells

00:45:35.937 --> 00:45:38.650
and the chemistry of the cells
that those cells talk to.

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:41.620
So that gives us one particular
category of signaling

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:44.940
from gut to brain, which
is chemical signaling.

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:47.560
But of course there are
other forms of signals

00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:51.550
and those fall under the
category of mechanical signaling.

00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:52.870
You're probably familiar with this.

00:45:52.870 --> 00:45:54.840
If you've ever eaten a very large meal

00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:56.740
or consumed a lot of fluid,

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:59.250
you experience that as
distension of the gut

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:02.360
and that doesn't just have to
be distension of the stomach,

00:46:02.360 --> 00:46:06.100
but distension of your intestines as well.

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:08.290
That distension is registered by neurons

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:09.910
that reside in your gut,

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:11.250
the signals go up to your brain,

00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:12.820
and communicate with areas of the brain

00:46:12.820 --> 00:46:14.920
that are responsible for suppressing

00:46:14.920 --> 00:46:17.740
further consumption of food and/or fluid,

00:46:17.740 --> 00:46:20.200
and, under certain circumstances,

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:22.940
can also be associated with the activation

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:24.820
of neural circuits that cause vomiting

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:26.070
or the desire to vomit.

00:46:26.070 --> 00:46:28.870
So if ever you've eaten too
much or you've eaten something

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:30.290
that doesn't agree with you,

00:46:30.290 --> 00:46:32.540
that information is communicated

00:46:32.540 --> 00:46:33.970
by way of mechanosensors

00:46:33.970 --> 00:46:36.210
that sense the mechanics of your gut,

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:37.910
possibly also the chemistry of your gut,

00:46:37.910 --> 00:46:40.010
but mostly the mechanics of your gut,

00:46:40.010 --> 00:46:41.520
signal up to the brain,

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:43.630
and activate brain
centers that are involved

00:46:43.630 --> 00:46:46.680
in stopping the eating behavior,

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:50.490
and activation of an
area of the brain stem

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:52.340
that is affectionately referred to

00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:55.090
as the vomit center among neuroanatomists.

00:46:55.090 --> 00:46:57.830
This is a area that more appropriately

00:46:57.830 --> 00:47:00.840
is called the chemoreceptor
trigger zone, the CTZ,

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:02.070
or area postrema

00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:04.840
and neurons in this area
actually will trigger

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:06.820
the vomiting reflex.

00:47:06.820 --> 00:47:10.130
So the way that the gut
and the brain communicate

00:47:10.130 --> 00:47:12.000
is both chemical and mechanical,

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:13.450
and it can be both for sake

00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:16.970
of increasing certain types of behavior.

00:47:16.970 --> 00:47:18.800
Today, we're talking mainly
about feeding behavior

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:20.470
up until now anyway

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:24.010
but also ceasing to
eat, closing your mouth,

00:47:24.010 --> 00:47:26.860
moving away from food,
turning away from food,

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:28.310
all behaviors that we're familiar with

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:29.610
anytime we feel kind of sick

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:33.500
on the basis of activation
of this mechanosensor

00:47:33.500 --> 00:47:35.540
for gastric distress.

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:38.390
So we've got chemical signaling
and mechanical signaling.

00:47:38.390 --> 00:47:40.490
And I also want to emphasize that we have

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:44.070
direct and indirect signaling
from the gut to the brain.

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:46.280
Direct signaling is the kind of signaling

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:48.760
of the sort I've been talking
about mainly up until now,

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:51.400
which is neurons in the gut

00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:53.190
communicating with
neurons in the brain stem

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:55.410
that communicate with
neurons in the hypothalamus.

00:47:55.410 --> 00:47:57.380
And, of course, those are
also going to interact

00:47:57.380 --> 00:47:59.500
with neurons of the prefrontal cortex

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:01.610
which is the area of a brain
involved in decision making

00:48:01.610 --> 00:48:05.260
the, you know, I think it was
the shrimp that made me sick.

00:48:05.260 --> 00:48:07.350
I'm going to, I just don't
want any more of that.

00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:10.170
Or I'm never going back
to that restaurant again

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:13.000
because after I ate there
about an hour later,

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.610
I started feeling really not well.

00:48:14.610 --> 00:48:15.990
I felt, you know, kind of feverish,

00:48:15.990 --> 00:48:17.090
but my gut didn't feel well,

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:19.140
my digestion was really off.

00:48:19.140 --> 00:48:20.310
All of that kind of information

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:23.760
is handled in the prefrontal
cortex at a conscious level,

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:26.030
but the immediate decision to stop eating

00:48:26.030 --> 00:48:28.300
or to eat more of something
to move towards something

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:30.430
or away from it, that's
made by neural circuits

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:32.730
that reside at the, we would say,

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:34.190
the subconscious level

00:48:34.190 --> 00:48:36.860
but what we really mean is below
the level of the neocortex.

00:48:36.860 --> 00:48:39.730
Below the cortex means
essentially below our level

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:41.030
of conscious awareness.

00:48:41.030 --> 00:48:43.700
So we talked about two types
of information within the gut

00:48:43.700 --> 00:48:45.350
that are communicated to the brain,

00:48:45.350 --> 00:48:47.340
chemical information, meaning information

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:49.130
about the nutrients
that happen to be there,

00:48:49.130 --> 00:48:49.963
and mechanical information,

00:48:49.963 --> 00:48:53.440
distention of the gut or lack
of distention and so forth.

00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:55.590
And we talked about how
these neuropod cells

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:57.460
can signal the release
of dopamine and circuits

00:48:57.460 --> 00:49:00.610
within the brain to cause you
to seek out more of something.

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:04.700
Now, in a very logically consistent way,

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:08.830
dopamine is also involved in
the whole business of vomiting.

00:49:08.830 --> 00:49:10.390
You might think, well, that
doesn't make any sense.

00:49:10.390 --> 00:49:12.140
I thought dopamine was
always a good thing.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:14.810
It's involved in moderation
and reward, et cetera.

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:16.580
But it turns out the area postrema,

00:49:16.580 --> 00:49:19.490
this vomit center in the brain stem,

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:22.130
is chockablock full of dopamine receptors.

00:49:22.130 --> 00:49:24.730
And if dopamine levels go too high,

00:49:24.730 --> 00:49:26.410
it can actually trigger vomiting.

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:29.520
And this we see in the
context of various drugs

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:31.620
that are used to treat
things like Parkinson's.

00:49:31.620 --> 00:49:34.053
Parkinson's is a deficiency in dopamine

00:49:34.053 --> 00:49:36.170
or a lack of dopamine neurons typically

00:49:36.170 --> 00:49:37.490
that causes a resting tremor,

00:49:37.490 --> 00:49:38.640
difficulty in movement,

00:49:38.640 --> 00:49:40.000
because dopamine's also associated

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:42.350
with a lot of the neural
circuits for movement.

00:49:43.190 --> 00:49:46.010
Many drugs that are used
to treat Parkinson's

00:49:46.010 --> 00:49:49.920
like L-DOPA increase
levels of dopamine so much,

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:52.880
or at least activate dopamine receptors

00:49:52.880 --> 00:49:55.950
to such a great degree in
certain areas of the brain

00:49:55.950 --> 00:49:58.410
that they can cause activation of things

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:01.750
like the trigger to vomit.

00:50:01.750 --> 00:50:04.240
Now, this should also make
sense in the natural context

00:50:04.240 --> 00:50:05.820
of if you gorge yourself with food,

00:50:05.820 --> 00:50:08.140
gorge yourself with food,
gorge yourself with food,

00:50:08.140 --> 00:50:11.220
the neurons in your gut
that respond to that

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:13.540
are simply detecting the
presence of nutrients

00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:16.380
but they don't really
make decisions themselves.

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:18.140
They don't know to stop eating.

00:50:18.140 --> 00:50:21.250
Your brain knows to stop
eating or to eject that food.

00:50:21.250 --> 00:50:23.610
And so it's a wonderful
thing that those neurons

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:25.610
are communicating with areas of the brain

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:27.930
not just that stimulate
consuming more food

00:50:27.930 --> 00:50:31.120
but that are communicating
with areas of the brain,

00:50:31.120 --> 00:50:32.780
for instance, area postrema,

00:50:32.780 --> 00:50:35.023
that when dopamine levels get too high,

00:50:35.970 --> 00:50:38.860
cause us to either stop eating that food

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:41.950
or in the case of vomiting
to eject that food.

00:50:41.950 --> 00:50:44.390
So I raise this not to give you

00:50:44.390 --> 00:50:46.770
a kind of a disgusting counterexample

00:50:46.770 --> 00:50:49.040
to what we call appetitive behaviors,

00:50:49.040 --> 00:50:51.120
the things that we like to do more of,

00:50:51.120 --> 00:50:54.490
but simply to give you a
sense of just how strongly

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:56.380
even these reflexes that we think of

00:50:56.380 --> 00:50:58.270
as feeling sick and vomiting

00:50:58.270 --> 00:51:00.260
or the desire to seek out more food

00:51:00.260 --> 00:51:03.230
are really being controlled
by a kind of push-pull system,

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:05.470
by parallel pathways that
are arriving from our gut

00:51:05.470 --> 00:51:08.190
and the same neurochemicals,
in this case dopamine,

00:51:08.190 --> 00:51:12.190
are being used to create
two opposite type behaviors,

00:51:12.190 --> 00:51:13.750
one behavior to consume more,

00:51:13.750 --> 00:51:15.240
one behavior to get rid of everything

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:16.510
you've already consumed.

00:51:16.510 --> 00:51:18.640
So our brain is actually sensitive

00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:21.440
to the amount of signaling
coming from our gut

00:51:21.440 --> 00:51:25.040
not just the path by
which that signal arrives.

00:51:25.040 --> 00:51:28.870
Our brain is very
carefully paying attention

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:30.450
to whether or not the levels of dopamine

00:51:30.450 --> 00:51:32.910
that are being triggered
are within a normal range

00:51:32.910 --> 00:51:34.260
for typical eating behavior

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:35.740
or whether or not we've gorged ourselves

00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:37.670
to the point where enough already.

00:51:37.670 --> 00:51:40.060
Now, of course, mechanical signals

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:43.750
will also play into area postrema

00:51:43.750 --> 00:51:45.020
and into the vomiting reflex.

00:51:45.020 --> 00:51:46.440
If we have a very distended gut,

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:47.300
we feel lousy.

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:49.530
It just, it actually can hurt very badly,

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:51.530
and we will have the desire to vomit,

00:51:51.530 --> 00:51:53.113
or we will just simply vomit.

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.590
Mechanical and chemical signals

00:51:56.590 --> 00:51:58.380
are always arriving in parallel.

00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:00.980
They never work in unison.

00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:04.700
And so now we have chemical
signals, mechanical signals,

00:52:04.700 --> 00:52:07.610
and now I'd like to talk about
direct and indirect signals

00:52:07.610 --> 00:52:10.640
because almost everything
I've talked about up until now

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:13.010
are direct signals, a neural
pathway that converges

00:52:13.010 --> 00:52:14.700
in the brain to create
a particular feeling,

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:15.950
thought, or behavior,

00:52:15.950 --> 00:52:18.320
but there are also indirect pathways.

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:21.270
And that's what takes us
back to the gut microbiome

00:52:21.270 --> 00:52:22.910
and to these little microbiota.

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:26.230
And to just give you the takeaway
message at the front here

00:52:26.230 --> 00:52:28.410
and then I'll give you
a little more detail

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:29.660
as to how it comes about,

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:33.870
you have neurotransmitters in your brain

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:35.580
and in your spinal cord and in your eyes

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:37.270
and in your peripheral nervous system.

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:39.850
They cause the activation
or the suppression

00:52:39.850 --> 00:52:41.130
of nerve activity,

00:52:41.130 --> 00:52:44.100
meaning they either electrically
activate other nerve cells

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:45.290
or they cause other nerve cells

00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:46.960
to be less electrically active.

00:52:46.960 --> 00:52:49.210
And they do that by way
of neurotransmitters.

00:52:50.150 --> 00:52:51.560
But as it turns out,

00:52:51.560 --> 00:52:53.950
the gut microbiota are capable

00:52:53.950 --> 00:52:55.780
of influencing metabolic events

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:57.400
and in some cases are capable

00:52:57.400 --> 00:53:00.230
of synthesizing
neurotransmitters themselves.

00:53:00.230 --> 00:53:02.540
So what that means is
that these little bugs,

00:53:02.540 --> 00:53:05.160
these little microbiota
that are cargo in your gut,

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:06.790
the six pounds of cargo,

00:53:06.790 --> 00:53:08.580
they actually can make neurochemicals

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:10.550
that can pass into the bloodstream

00:53:10.550 --> 00:53:12.480
and into your brain and actually impact

00:53:12.480 --> 00:53:15.570
the other cells of your
body and brain indirectly,

00:53:15.570 --> 00:53:18.130
so without involving these
very intricate nerve pathways

00:53:18.130 --> 00:53:19.740
that we've been talking about.

00:53:19.740 --> 00:53:22.483
In other words, the foods you eat,

00:53:23.490 --> 00:53:25.800
the environment of your gut microbiome,

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:27.950
can actually create
the chemical substrates

00:53:27.950 --> 00:53:30.360
that allow your brain to
feel one way or the other,

00:53:30.360 --> 00:53:32.200
to feel great or to feel lousy,

00:53:32.200 --> 00:53:34.950
to seek out more of a
particular type of behavior

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:36.360
or to avoid that behavior.

00:53:36.360 --> 00:53:38.800
And that would constitute
indirect signaling.

00:53:38.800 --> 00:53:41.360
So I've been talking a lot
about the structure and function

00:53:41.360 --> 00:53:43.030
of the gut-to-brain pathway,

00:53:43.030 --> 00:53:45.880
focusing mainly on feeding behaviors

00:53:45.880 --> 00:53:49.140
and in some cases avoiding
feeding or even ejecting food

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:51.070
from the digestive tract,

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:52.790
I'd like to drill a little bit deeper

00:53:52.790 --> 00:53:55.160
into this indirect signaling pathway

00:53:55.160 --> 00:53:56.360
from the gut to the brain

00:53:56.360 --> 00:54:00.080
because it bridges us
nicely from neuronal signals

00:54:00.080 --> 00:54:01.500
in the gut to the brain,

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:04.070
hormonal signals from
the gut to the brain,

00:54:04.070 --> 00:54:06.570
to what also includes the microbiome,

00:54:06.570 --> 00:54:08.070
which is what we started talking about

00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:09.670
at the beginning of the episode.

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:12.720
As I mentioned a couple of minutes ago,

00:54:14.270 --> 00:54:17.010
certain gut microbiota
can actually synthesize

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:19.710
certain neurotransmitters
that can go impact the brain.

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:22.920
And we actually have some
knowledge about which microbiota

00:54:22.920 --> 00:54:25.240
can synthesize particular
neurotransmitters.

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:28.240
For instance, the neuromodulator dopamine

00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:29.360
can be synthesized

00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:34.090
by or from bacillus and serratia.

00:54:34.090 --> 00:54:36.300
Now, these are just names of microbiota.

00:54:36.300 --> 00:54:37.750
I don't expect that any of you

00:54:37.750 --> 00:54:39.110
would necessarily recognize them.

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:40.590
These aren't the sorts of
things that you necessarily

00:54:40.590 --> 00:54:43.190
would run out and buy
to get more dopamine.

00:54:43.190 --> 00:54:47.240
But the point is that
particular gut microbiota

00:54:47.240 --> 00:54:49.790
can create dopamine in our gut

00:54:49.790 --> 00:54:51.350
that can get into our bloodstream

00:54:51.350 --> 00:54:55.150
and can generally change our
baseline levels of dopamine

00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:58.210
within the brain and
other areas of the body.

00:54:58.210 --> 00:55:00.040
I mentioned baseline levels of dopamine

00:55:00.040 --> 00:55:03.530
because as I talked about on
an episode all about dopamine

00:55:03.530 --> 00:55:07.170
but I'll just repeat the basics here now,

00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:08.560
we have baseline levels

00:55:08.560 --> 00:55:10.990
of transmitters or neuromodulators

00:55:10.990 --> 00:55:13.630
that act as sort of the level of the tide,

00:55:13.630 --> 00:55:14.940
the overall level,

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:16.880
and then we can have peaks of dopamine

00:55:16.880 --> 00:55:18.440
that are created by behaviors

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:21.710
or by ingestion of particular
foods or drugs, et cetera.

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:25.420
So bacillus and serratia tend to increase

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:28.150
our baseline levels of dopamine.

00:55:28.150 --> 00:55:30.900
So if it turns out that we are creating

00:55:30.900 --> 00:55:33.810
the right gut microbiome environment

00:55:33.810 --> 00:55:38.010
that these particular gut
microbiota can thrive in,

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:41.400
well, then our baseline levels
of dopamine will be elevated

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:43.900
and in general, that leads
to enhancement of mood.

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:48.630
Similarly, there are other gut microbiota,

00:55:48.630 --> 00:55:53.290
for instance, candida,
streptococus, various enterococcus,

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:54.980
these always have these kind of strange

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:57.090
and not so attractive
names, at least to me

00:55:57.090 --> 00:55:58.440
as a neurobiologist.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.860
Nonetheless, those particular microbiota

00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:05.600
support the production of
or can even be metabolized

00:56:05.600 --> 00:56:08.850
into serotonin, which is a neuromodulator

00:56:08.850 --> 00:56:12.020
associated with mood,
with social interactions,

00:56:12.020 --> 00:56:14.000
with a huge number of different types

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:15.810
of events and behaviors.

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:20.240
Again, these gut microbiota when present

00:56:20.240 --> 00:56:22.030
and allowed to thrive in our gut

00:56:22.030 --> 00:56:25.670
will increase our overall
levels of serotonin

00:56:25.670 --> 00:56:29.020
and riding on top of
that level of serotonin

00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:31.730
will be the serotonin
that's specifically released

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:33.670
in response to certain behaviors.

00:56:33.670 --> 00:56:35.980
And I really want to drive home this point

00:56:35.980 --> 00:56:37.730
of baselines and peaks.

00:56:37.730 --> 00:56:40.790
The baseline level of serotonin
might set our overall mood,

00:56:40.790 --> 00:56:42.947
whether or not we wake
up feeling pretty good

00:56:42.947 --> 00:56:45.790
or really lousy if our serotonin levels

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:47.420
happen to be very, very low,

00:56:47.420 --> 00:56:50.130
whether or not we tend to
be in a kind of a calm space

00:56:50.130 --> 00:56:52.530
or whether or not we tend
to be somewhat irritable.

00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:56.080
But then of course individual
events as we go about our day,

00:56:56.080 --> 00:56:57.490
maybe a compliment that we get

00:56:57.490 --> 00:56:59.900
or maybe somebody says
something irritating to us,

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:02.920
whatever it may be will also
influence levels of serotonin,

00:57:02.920 --> 00:57:05.830
but those serotonin events
are going to be related

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:08.920
to events at particular
neural circuits in the brain.

00:57:08.920 --> 00:57:10.540
And this is an important topic

00:57:10.540 --> 00:57:14.130
because I think that a lot of
people hear quite accurately,

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:18.400
oh, 90 to 95% of our serotonin
is manufactured in the gut.

00:57:18.400 --> 00:57:19.320
And indeed that's true.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.810
It's manufactured from
the sorts of microbiota

00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:23.740
that I just described.

00:57:23.740 --> 00:57:25.180
And there are many, many experiments now,

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:26.320
mostly in animal models,

00:57:26.320 --> 00:57:30.360
but also some in humans that
show that if the gut microbiome

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:34.920
is deficient in some way to
these particular bacteria,

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:37.460
that serotonin levels drop
and people's mood suffers,

00:57:37.460 --> 00:57:39.520
maybe even their immune system functions,

00:57:39.520 --> 00:57:42.423
maybe even it exacerbates
certain psychiatric illnesses.

00:57:43.880 --> 00:57:45.660
However, a lot of people take that to mean

00:57:45.660 --> 00:57:50.130
that the serotonin of the
brain all comes from the gut

00:57:50.130 --> 00:57:51.600
or mostly comes from the gut.

00:57:51.600 --> 00:57:52.433
That's not the case.

00:57:52.433 --> 00:57:54.870
It's still the case that you
have neurons in the brain

00:57:54.870 --> 00:57:57.260
that are responsible
for releasing serotonin

00:57:57.260 --> 00:58:00.200
directly in response to certain
things like social touch

00:58:00.200 --> 00:58:04.160
or through other types of
positive social experiences.

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:07.550
So we've got gut microbiota

00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:10.120
that can literally be turned into dopamine

00:58:10.120 --> 00:58:11.840
and raise our baseline levels of dopamine.

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:13.010
We've got gut microbiota

00:58:13.010 --> 00:58:16.410
that can literally raise our
baseline levels of serotonin.

00:58:16.410 --> 00:58:19.770
And indeed there are other gut
microbiota like lactobacillus

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:23.980
or bifidobacterium, excuse me,

00:58:23.980 --> 00:58:26.140
hard complex names to pronounce,

00:58:26.140 --> 00:58:29.070
bifidobacterium that can give rise

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:30.920
to increases in GABA levels,

00:58:30.920 --> 00:58:32.410
this inhibitory neurotransmitter

00:58:32.410 --> 00:58:34.780
that can act as a little
bit of a mild sedative,

00:58:34.780 --> 00:58:37.280
can reduce irritability, et cetera,

00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:38.960
but that's just the baseline,

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:41.320
the kind of tide of those neuromodulators.

00:58:41.320 --> 00:58:42.543
Again, I want to emphasize
that we still have

00:58:42.543 --> 00:58:44.700
neurocircuits within the brain and body

00:58:44.700 --> 00:58:47.900
that are specifically
releasing in a very potent way

00:58:47.900 --> 00:58:50.120
dopamine, serotonin, and GABA.

00:58:50.120 --> 00:58:52.310
So the two things act in concert.

00:58:52.310 --> 00:58:54.670
Even though the gut and
the brain are acting

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:59.130
both in parallel and directly
influencing one another,

00:58:59.130 --> 00:59:01.860
it is a powerful synergistic effect.

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:04.270
And there are now hundreds of studies,

00:59:04.270 --> 00:59:06.860
maybe even thousands by this point,

00:59:06.860 --> 00:59:09.560
mostly performed in animal models,

00:59:09.560 --> 00:59:12.290
typically mice, but also
some studies in humans

00:59:12.290 --> 00:59:15.590
that show that creating
the correct environment

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:17.350
for these gut microbiota to thrive

00:59:17.350 --> 00:59:20.600
really does enhance mood and wellbeing.

00:59:20.600 --> 00:59:25.240
And that when our gut
microbiome is not healthy,

00:59:25.240 --> 00:59:28.830
that it really can deplete our
mood and sense of wellbeing.

00:59:28.830 --> 00:59:31.540
Now, there are two major phases

00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:33.950
to creating a healthy gut microbiome.

00:59:33.950 --> 00:59:36.150
One you can control and the other one

00:59:36.150 --> 00:59:37.763
is less under your control.

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:40.890
I get into this in a lot of detail

00:59:40.890 --> 00:59:42.780
in the episode with Dr. Sonnenburg,

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:46.560
which is coming out
immediately after this one,

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:48.540
the following Monday, that is.

00:59:48.540 --> 00:59:52.470
But for now I want to just
capture a few of the main points

00:59:52.470 --> 00:59:55.483
about the early establishment
of the gut microbiome.

00:59:56.720 --> 00:59:59.760
It turns out that the environment

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:01.110
that we are exposed to,

01:00:01.110 --> 01:00:02.850
the things that come into
contact with our skin

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:05.480
and digestive tract and
any other mucosal lining,

01:00:05.480 --> 01:00:08.420
even the urethra, the nasal passages,

01:00:08.420 --> 01:00:11.020
any opening to the outside world

01:00:11.020 --> 01:00:13.640
that brings in certain, excuse me,

01:00:13.640 --> 01:00:17.850
certain microbiota in the
first three years of life

01:00:17.850 --> 01:00:20.160
is going to have a profound impact

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:22.960
on the overall menu of microbiota

01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:27.330
that we will be able to
carry within our body.

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:30.360
And it really does seem
that getting exposure to

01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:32.350
and building a diverse microbiome

01:00:32.350 --> 01:00:34.930
in those first three years is critical.

01:00:34.930 --> 01:00:37.610
There's a lot of speculation and some data

01:00:37.610 --> 01:00:40.550
as to cesarean delivered babies

01:00:40.550 --> 01:00:43.140
having less diverse microbiomes

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:44.800
compared to vaginally delivered babies.

01:00:44.800 --> 01:00:45.990
There have been attempts,

01:00:45.990 --> 01:00:48.020
although not conclusive attempts,

01:00:48.020 --> 01:00:51.640
to link that to the presence
of autism spectrum disorders,

01:00:51.640 --> 01:00:53.620
which at least by some statistics

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:57.820
seem to be of higher probability
in cesarean deliveries

01:00:57.820 --> 01:01:00.450
although there are other
studies that refute that,

01:01:00.450 --> 01:01:01.850
and I want to make that clear.

01:01:03.110 --> 01:01:06.270
However, it's clear that
babies do not get much,

01:01:06.270 --> 01:01:10.470
if any, exposure to
microbiota inside of the womb,

01:01:10.470 --> 01:01:11.920
maybe a little bit, but not much.

01:01:11.920 --> 01:01:13.690
But that is during the birth process

01:01:13.690 --> 01:01:17.300
and in the days and
weeks immediately after

01:01:17.300 --> 01:01:19.970
they arrive in the world
that their gut microbiome

01:01:19.970 --> 01:01:22.580
is established, that those gut microbiota

01:01:22.580 --> 01:01:24.410
take residence within the gut.

01:01:24.410 --> 01:01:26.500
So it will depend on whether
or not they were breastfed

01:01:26.500 --> 01:01:27.400
or bottle fed.

01:01:27.400 --> 01:01:29.460
It'll depend on whether
or not they were exposed

01:01:29.460 --> 01:01:30.820
to a household pet or not,

01:01:30.820 --> 01:01:33.400
whether or not they were
held by multiple caregivers

01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:34.300
or just by one,

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:36.220
whether or not they were a preemie baby

01:01:36.220 --> 01:01:39.730
and were contained in a
particularly restrictive environment

01:01:39.730 --> 01:01:41.570
in order to encourage
their further development

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:43.730
before they could be brought home or not.

01:01:43.730 --> 01:01:46.750
I don't want to give the picture
that if you were isolated

01:01:46.750 --> 01:01:48.790
or you were delivered by C-section,

01:01:48.790 --> 01:01:50.830
that you're somehow doomed
to have a poor microbiome.

01:01:50.830 --> 01:01:52.560
That's simply not the case.

01:01:52.560 --> 01:01:55.570
However, it is the case
that the more diversity

01:01:55.570 --> 01:02:00.090
of microbiota that one
can create early in life

01:02:00.090 --> 01:02:03.410
is really helpful for long-term outcomes

01:02:03.410 --> 01:02:06.460
in terms of brain-to-gut signaling,

01:02:06.460 --> 01:02:07.870
gut-to-brain signaling,

01:02:07.870 --> 01:02:10.963
and for sake of the immune system.

01:02:11.830 --> 01:02:14.180
There are some decent studies showing

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:16.330
that if children are exposed

01:02:16.330 --> 01:02:19.600
to a lot of antibiotic
treatment early in life,

01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:21.970
that can be very
detrimental to establishment

01:02:21.970 --> 01:02:23.800
of a healthy gut microbiome.

01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:27.320
And fortunately that reestablishing
a healthy gut microbiome

01:02:27.320 --> 01:02:29.710
can help rescue some of those deficits.

01:02:29.710 --> 01:02:32.570
So doctors nowadays are much more cautious

01:02:32.570 --> 01:02:36.200
about the prescription of
antibiotic drugs to children

01:02:36.200 --> 01:02:38.260
in their early years, not
just up to three years,

01:02:38.260 --> 01:02:41.020
but extending out to five
and seven and 10 years.

01:02:41.020 --> 01:02:44.620
And even in adults, they're
very, very careful about that,

01:02:44.620 --> 01:02:46.160
or they ought to be.

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:49.360
One reason is the
existence, or I would say,

01:02:49.360 --> 01:02:52.990
the proliferation of
antibiotic-resistant bacteria

01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:55.800
that are becoming more common
in hospitals and elsewhere

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:57.540
and that can cause serious problems.

01:02:57.540 --> 01:02:58.470
But in addition to that,

01:02:58.470 --> 01:03:00.980
because of this understanding
that the gut microbiome

01:03:00.980 --> 01:03:04.030
is influencing and actually
creating neurotransmitters

01:03:04.030 --> 01:03:06.010
that can impact mood and mental health,

01:03:06.010 --> 01:03:08.180
impact immune health, and so on.

01:03:08.180 --> 01:03:09.790
As I mentioned earlier, there are hundreds

01:03:09.790 --> 01:03:12.930
if not thousands of studies
emphasizing the key role

01:03:12.930 --> 01:03:15.530
of the microbiome on brain health,

01:03:15.530 --> 01:03:18.080
psychiatric health, et cetera.

01:03:18.080 --> 01:03:20.610
I want to just highlight
a few of those studies

01:03:20.610 --> 01:03:23.920
and in particular, some recent
studies that come from labs

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:25.090
that have been working
on this sort of thing

01:03:25.090 --> 01:03:26.650
for a very long time.

01:03:26.650 --> 01:03:28.900
One of the more exciting
studies comes from the work

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:31.120
of Mauro Costa-Mattioli's lab,

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:33.240
which is at Baylor College of Medicine.

01:03:33.240 --> 01:03:36.200
Mauro's lab has been
working on mouse models

01:03:36.200 --> 01:03:39.190
of autism spectrum
disorder for a long time,

01:03:39.190 --> 01:03:41.320
and looking at social behavior

01:03:41.320 --> 01:03:43.550
using a mouse model for a long time.

01:03:43.550 --> 01:03:46.260
And they've been able to identify

01:03:46.260 --> 01:03:49.050
particular types of microbiota

01:03:49.050 --> 01:03:52.460
that when they take resonance in the gut

01:03:52.460 --> 01:03:56.520
can help offset some of
the symptoms of autism,

01:03:56.520 --> 01:03:57.870
at least the symptoms of autism

01:03:57.870 --> 01:03:59.520
that exist in these mouse models, okay?

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:01.440
So again, this is not human work.

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:03.050
This is work being done on mouse models

01:04:03.050 --> 01:04:04.900
for the simple reason that you can do

01:04:04.900 --> 01:04:06.070
these kinds of manipulations,

01:04:06.070 --> 01:04:07.920
where basically they took mice

01:04:07.920 --> 01:04:09.330
that were in germ free-environments

01:04:09.330 --> 01:04:11.610
or non-germ-free environments,

01:04:11.610 --> 01:04:14.010
or they exposed mice to
particular microbiota

01:04:14.010 --> 01:04:15.440
and not other microbiota

01:04:15.440 --> 01:04:18.730
and they discovered that
a particular microbiota

01:04:18.730 --> 01:04:19.970
called L. reuteri,

01:04:19.970 --> 01:04:24.090
it's L. R-E-U-T-E-R-I.

01:04:24.090 --> 01:04:28.410
Treatment with L. reuteri
corrects the social deficits

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:30.200
present in these autism models

01:04:30.200 --> 01:04:33.020
and it does so by way of
activating our old friend

01:04:33.020 --> 01:04:34.340
the vagus nerve,

01:04:34.340 --> 01:04:36.620
but not simply because the vagus nerve

01:04:36.620 --> 01:04:38.280
triggers the release of dopamine,

01:04:38.280 --> 01:04:41.140
but it turns out that this
particular gut microbiota

01:04:41.140 --> 01:04:43.670
L. reuteri can correct the social deficits

01:04:43.670 --> 01:04:45.990
in this autism spectrum disorder model.

01:04:45.990 --> 01:04:48.270
It does that by way of
a vagal nerve pathway

01:04:48.270 --> 01:04:51.020
that stimulates both dopamine
release and oxytocin release.

01:04:51.020 --> 01:04:53.000
And they establish this
really mechanistically

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:53.833
by showing, for instance,

01:04:53.833 --> 01:04:55.920
if you get rid of the oxytocin receptor,

01:04:55.920 --> 01:04:57.520
you don't see this rescue.

01:04:57.520 --> 01:04:58.660
Now, those are mouse models

01:04:58.660 --> 01:05:01.750
so we have to take those with
the appropriate grain of salt,

01:05:01.750 --> 01:05:02.810
but they're really exciting.

01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:06.880
And they come to us in
parallel with other studies

01:05:06.880 --> 01:05:07.800
that are being done,

01:05:07.800 --> 01:05:11.160
taking the microbiomes of people

01:05:11.160 --> 01:05:13.810
who have one condition
or lack of condition,

01:05:13.810 --> 01:05:16.510
and putting it into people
who have one condition

01:05:16.510 --> 01:05:17.440
or another condition.

01:05:17.440 --> 01:05:19.190
Let me explain what I mean by that.

01:05:20.320 --> 01:05:22.370
The early discovery of the gut microbiome

01:05:22.370 --> 01:05:25.270
and its potential to impact health

01:05:25.270 --> 01:05:28.510
was not in the context of
the gut-to-brain pathway

01:05:28.510 --> 01:05:30.830
but rather it was in
the context of colitis.

01:05:30.830 --> 01:05:33.360
This dates back to studies in the '50s,

01:05:33.360 --> 01:05:36.950
whereby people with very
severe intractable colitis

01:05:36.950 --> 01:05:39.150
for which no other
treatment was going to work

01:05:39.150 --> 01:05:40.870
received fecal transplants.

01:05:40.870 --> 01:05:42.750
So yes, that's exactly as it sounds.

01:05:42.750 --> 01:05:46.170
Taking the stool of healthy
people who do not have colitis,

01:05:46.170 --> 01:05:49.610
transplanting those stools
into the lower digestive tract

01:05:49.610 --> 01:05:51.370
of people who do have colitis,

01:05:51.370 --> 01:05:53.930
and they saw significant improvement,

01:05:53.930 --> 01:05:55.980
if not rescue of the colitis.

01:05:55.980 --> 01:05:57.450
That was one of the first indications

01:05:57.450 --> 01:06:00.850
that something within
stool, of all things,

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:04.750
could actually rescue another
individual from disease,

01:06:04.750 --> 01:06:07.080
which sounds kind of wild and crazy,

01:06:07.080 --> 01:06:09.410
and may even sound
disgusting to some of you,

01:06:09.410 --> 01:06:12.960
but as I mentioned at the
beginning of the episode,

01:06:12.960 --> 01:06:17.740
almost 60% of stool is
live or dead bacteria,

01:06:17.740 --> 01:06:21.780
microbiota, and it really
opened up this entire field

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:23.818
of exploring how different microbiota

01:06:23.818 --> 01:06:26.390
might have therapeutic effects.

01:06:26.390 --> 01:06:28.930
And indeed, that has
been shown to be the case

01:06:28.930 --> 01:06:32.940
also in fecal transplants for
certain psychiatric illnesses.

01:06:32.940 --> 01:06:35.310
These are still ongoing studies.

01:06:35.310 --> 01:06:37.590
They vary in quality.

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:40.420
These are hard studies to
do for all sorts of reasons,

01:06:40.420 --> 01:06:42.170
getting the appropriate
patient populations,

01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:43.620
getting agreement, et cetera,

01:06:44.550 --> 01:06:46.840
making sure that everything's
handled properly.

01:06:46.840 --> 01:06:49.390
But what this involves
is fecal transplants

01:06:49.390 --> 01:06:51.443
from individuals that lack

01:06:51.443 --> 01:06:53.210
a particular psychiatric condition

01:06:53.210 --> 01:06:55.620
or metabolic condition into people

01:06:55.620 --> 01:06:58.010
who have a particular metabolic condition

01:06:58.010 --> 01:07:00.710
and there has been tremendous
success in some cases.

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:03.640
One of the more powerful
and salient examples

01:07:03.640 --> 01:07:04.980
is for obesity.

01:07:04.980 --> 01:07:06.930
There's some people for
which even if they ingest

01:07:06.930 --> 01:07:09.460
very low numbers of calories,

01:07:09.460 --> 01:07:11.480
even if they go on a liquid protein diet,

01:07:11.480 --> 01:07:13.720
simply can't lose weight.

01:07:13.720 --> 01:07:14.960
These are somewhat rare disorders

01:07:14.960 --> 01:07:16.160
but these are people that would

01:07:16.160 --> 01:07:18.360
either get gastric bypass surgery.

01:07:18.360 --> 01:07:20.420
Some people are now getting
these fecal transplants

01:07:20.420 --> 01:07:22.150
from people that have

01:07:23.126 --> 01:07:28.126
healthy weight and they
take the stool from them,

01:07:28.530 --> 01:07:30.420
they put it into lower digestive tract,

01:07:30.420 --> 01:07:34.400
and they can see substantial
improvement in weight loss

01:07:34.400 --> 01:07:36.500
in people that were
otherwise unable to do that.

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:39.020
In some cases, actually
they can start eating

01:07:39.020 --> 01:07:41.290
relatively normal levels of
food and still lose weight.

01:07:41.290 --> 01:07:42.290
So pretty remarkable

01:07:42.290 --> 01:07:44.500
and that tells us there's
something in these microbiota

01:07:44.500 --> 01:07:45.900
that's really powerful.

01:07:45.900 --> 01:07:49.150
Now, how those effects
are generated isn't clear.

01:07:49.150 --> 01:07:53.200
One idea is that it's
impacting the metabolome,

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:54.620
components of the metabolism,

01:07:54.620 --> 01:07:56.610
almost certainly that's
going to be the case.

01:07:56.610 --> 01:07:59.410
Another idea is that it's
impacting neurotransmitters

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:02.490
which change behavior and
food choices within the brain.

01:08:02.490 --> 01:08:03.800
Although, as I mentioned,
some of these people

01:08:03.800 --> 01:08:06.750
are already eating very
little food to begin with

01:08:06.750 --> 01:08:10.740
so that's a little bit harder
of an argument to create.

01:08:10.740 --> 01:08:13.210
There are also some
somewhat famous examples now

01:08:13.210 --> 01:08:16.803
of how fecal transplants can
lead to negative outcomes.

01:08:17.670 --> 01:08:19.440
But those negative
outcomes further underscore

01:08:19.440 --> 01:08:23.313
the power of the microbiome
in impacting bodily health.

01:08:24.530 --> 01:08:26.750
One key example of this, for instance,

01:08:26.750 --> 01:08:31.090
is transfer of fecal
matter into another person

01:08:31.090 --> 01:08:33.260
in order to treat something like colitis

01:08:33.260 --> 01:08:35.870
and it effectively does that,

01:08:35.870 --> 01:08:39.630
but if the donor of the
stool, of the fecal matter

01:08:40.670 --> 01:08:43.730
happened to be obese or have
some other metabolic syndrome,

01:08:43.730 --> 01:08:45.470
it's been observed that the recipient

01:08:45.470 --> 01:08:47.940
can also develop that metabolic syndrome

01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:50.370
simply by way of receiving

01:08:50.370 --> 01:08:53.320
that donor's particular microbiota.

01:08:53.320 --> 01:08:55.860
So these microbiota can
create positive outcomes

01:08:55.860 --> 01:08:57.990
or they can create negative outcomes.

01:08:57.990 --> 01:09:00.330
Now, most of us of course,
are not interested in

01:09:00.330 --> 01:09:01.760
or pursuing fecal transplants.

01:09:01.760 --> 01:09:03.590
Most people are interested
in just creating

01:09:03.590 --> 01:09:05.750
a healthy gut microbiome environment

01:09:05.750 --> 01:09:07.600
for sake of immune system
and brain function.

01:09:07.600 --> 01:09:10.760
And we will talk about how to
do that in just a few minutes.

01:09:10.760 --> 01:09:13.470
But I just want to further underscore

01:09:13.470 --> 01:09:17.980
the power of the microbiota
in shaping brain chemistry

01:09:17.980 --> 01:09:20.170
and in shaping things like mood

01:09:20.170 --> 01:09:21.540
or other aspects of mental health

01:09:21.540 --> 01:09:24.470
that typically we don't
associate with our gut.

01:09:24.470 --> 01:09:26.780
There are several studies
published in recent years,

01:09:26.780 --> 01:09:28.240
one that I'll just highlight now,

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:32.500
first author is Tonya Nguyen, N-G-U-Y-E-N.

01:09:32.500 --> 01:09:33.567
The title of the paper is

01:09:33.567 --> 01:09:36.230
"Association of Loneliness and Wisdom

01:09:36.230 --> 01:09:38.870
with Gut Microbial
Diversity and Composition,

01:09:38.870 --> 01:09:40.350
an Exploratory Study".

01:09:40.350 --> 01:09:41.570
It's an interesting study.

01:09:41.570 --> 01:09:46.000
Looked at 184 community
dwelling adults, excuse me,

01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:49.220
ranging from 28 to 97 years old.

01:09:49.220 --> 01:09:52.160
They explored whether or not

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:54.630
having enhanced microbial diversity

01:09:54.630 --> 01:09:56.810
somehow related to these variables

01:09:56.810 --> 01:09:58.640
that they refer to as
loneliness and wisdom.

01:09:58.640 --> 01:10:02.180
They used a number of different
tests to evaluate those.

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:04.640
Those are common tests in
the psychology literature,

01:10:04.640 --> 01:10:06.420
not so much in the biology literature,

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:08.950
but nonetheless, there are
ways of measuring things

01:10:08.950 --> 01:10:10.833
like loneliness and wisdom,

01:10:12.140 --> 01:10:16.550
wisdom in this case, being
the opposite of loneliness,

01:10:16.550 --> 01:10:18.080
at least in the context of this study.

01:10:18.080 --> 01:10:20.700
And what they found was the
more microbial diversity,

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:22.970
the more diverse one's microbiome was,

01:10:22.970 --> 01:10:25.870
the lower incidence of loneliness.

01:10:25.870 --> 01:10:28.140
And they did this by taking fecal samples,

01:10:28.140 --> 01:10:29.530
profiling them for RNA.

01:10:29.530 --> 01:10:31.670
So essentially doing gene
sequencing of the stool

01:10:31.670 --> 01:10:32.800
of these individuals,

01:10:32.800 --> 01:10:35.760
getting ratings of how lonely
or not lonely they felt,

01:10:35.760 --> 01:10:37.060
and correlating those.

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:38.760
And that's just but one study.

01:10:38.760 --> 01:10:41.220
I point it out because
it's particularly recent

01:10:41.220 --> 01:10:43.650
and it looked like it was
particularly well done.

01:10:43.650 --> 01:10:45.610
There is another study that
I'll just refer you to.

01:10:45.610 --> 01:10:49.270
This was a study published in
2020 in "Scientific Reports".

01:10:49.270 --> 01:10:51.120
The title of the study
is "Emotional Wellbeing

01:10:51.120 --> 01:10:54.270
and Gut Microbiome
Profiles by Enterotype".

01:10:54.270 --> 01:10:55.770
What I particularly like about this study

01:10:55.770 --> 01:10:57.800
is that they were able
to correlate the presence

01:10:57.800 --> 01:11:02.650
of certain microbiota with
feelings of subjective wellbeing

01:11:02.650 --> 01:11:05.163
and lack of or presence
of depressive symptoms.

01:11:06.590 --> 01:11:08.810
They did high-throughput gene sequencing

01:11:08.810 --> 01:11:10.560
of the microbiomes of individuals.

01:11:10.560 --> 01:11:13.480
So that meant measuring the microbiota,

01:11:13.480 --> 01:11:15.670
figuring out which
microbiota were present,

01:11:15.670 --> 01:11:18.090
how diverse their
microbiome was in general.

01:11:18.090 --> 01:11:20.810
Gut microbiome diversity is a good thing.

01:11:20.810 --> 01:11:23.060
And then to correlate
that with what's called

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:26.270
the PANAS score.

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:29.920
PANAS stands for positive
affect negative affect schedule.

01:11:29.920 --> 01:11:31.930
This is a test that my
lab has used extensively,

01:11:31.930 --> 01:11:34.870
that other labs to use to
evaluate mood and wellbeing.

01:11:34.870 --> 01:11:38.650
And they defined what were
called three enterotypes,

01:11:38.650 --> 01:11:40.130
three different categories of people

01:11:40.130 --> 01:11:42.170
that ate very different diets

01:11:42.170 --> 01:11:44.850
that tended to fall into
categories of having more

01:11:44.850 --> 01:11:48.880
or fewer emotional
symptoms that were negative

01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:52.090
or more fewer emotional
symptoms that were positive

01:11:52.090 --> 01:11:55.300
and whether or not they tend
to be more depressed, anxious,

01:11:55.300 --> 01:11:57.800
or have more stress-related
behaviors, et cetera.

01:11:57.800 --> 01:12:01.010
And what they were able
to derive from this study

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:04.170
was some strong indications
about what types of things

01:12:04.170 --> 01:12:05.970
we should ingest in our diet,

01:12:05.970 --> 01:12:07.740
maybe even certain things
that we should avoid,

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:10.130
but certainly the types of
things that we should ingest,

01:12:10.130 --> 01:12:12.450
that can enhance mood and wellbeing

01:12:12.450 --> 01:12:14.670
and can tend to shift people away

01:12:14.670 --> 01:12:16.860
from more depressive-like anxiety

01:12:16.860 --> 01:12:18.280
and stress-related symptoms.

01:12:18.280 --> 01:12:20.960
Before we get into what the
particular food items were

01:12:20.960 --> 01:12:23.780
that lend themselves to
a healthy microbiome,

01:12:23.780 --> 01:12:25.770
I want to raise a bigger and
perhaps more important issue,

01:12:25.770 --> 01:12:28.513
which is what is a healthy microbiome.

01:12:29.820 --> 01:12:32.110
I think if you asked any
number of world experts,

01:12:32.110 --> 01:12:33.890
and I certainly ask
this of Dr. Sonnenburg,

01:12:33.890 --> 01:12:35.490
what is a healthy microbiome,

01:12:35.490 --> 01:12:36.430
they're all going to tell you

01:12:36.430 --> 01:12:38.980
it's a microbiome that
has a lot of diversity,

01:12:38.980 --> 01:12:41.857
that includes a lot of
different types of bacteria.

01:12:41.857 --> 01:12:43.140
And that makes sense

01:12:43.140 --> 01:12:46.490
because it logically
would include the bacteria

01:12:46.490 --> 01:12:48.880
that produce GABA and
dopamine and serotonin,

01:12:48.880 --> 01:12:50.470
and that support the immune system,

01:12:50.470 --> 01:12:51.930
and do a number of different things.

01:12:51.930 --> 01:12:56.410
But is it simply the case that
adding microbiota diversity

01:12:56.410 --> 01:12:58.480
is always a good thing?

01:12:58.480 --> 01:13:00.793
Well, that doesn't seem to be the case.

01:13:02.230 --> 01:13:04.050
Probiotics and prebiotics,

01:13:04.050 --> 01:13:07.010
both of which can enhance
microbiotal diversity,

01:13:07.010 --> 01:13:10.690
can improve mood, digestion,
immune system, and so on.

01:13:10.690 --> 01:13:12.990
That's been established but
it's mainly been established

01:13:12.990 --> 01:13:15.910
in the context of
post-antibiotic treatment

01:13:15.910 --> 01:13:17.740
or people that are recovering from illness

01:13:17.740 --> 01:13:20.940
or people that have been very stressed

01:13:20.940 --> 01:13:24.050
or have been dealing with
all sorts of challenges,

01:13:24.050 --> 01:13:25.320
mental or physical,

01:13:25.320 --> 01:13:28.950
and they are an attempt to
replenish the gut microbiome.

01:13:28.950 --> 01:13:32.310
However, it's also clear

01:13:32.310 --> 01:13:35.470
that excessive microbiota

01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:38.720
brought about by excessive
intake of probiotics

01:13:38.720 --> 01:13:40.280
can lead to things like brain fog.

01:13:40.280 --> 01:13:42.850
There's actually some good
studies that point to the fact

01:13:42.850 --> 01:13:46.420
that certain metabolites
of the microbiome,

01:13:46.420 --> 01:13:48.320
certain chemicals produced in the gut

01:13:48.320 --> 01:13:51.890
and in the body can actually
lead to brain fog states.

01:13:51.890 --> 01:13:55.030
This is thought to come about
through the lactate pathways

01:13:55.030 --> 01:13:58.000
of the gut that can then impact the brain.

01:13:58.000 --> 01:14:00.130
If you want to look more into this issue

01:14:00.130 --> 01:14:04.360
of whether or not probiotics
taken in excess perhaps

01:14:04.360 --> 01:14:06.550
can lead to brain fog,

01:14:06.550 --> 01:14:08.370
I'd encourage you to look
at a particular paper.

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:09.330
This is a paper published

01:14:09.330 --> 01:14:12.820
in "Clinical and Translational
Gastroenterology".

01:14:12.820 --> 01:14:13.653
And the title of the paper

01:14:13.653 --> 01:14:15.610
is "Brain Fogginess, Gas, and Bloating,

01:14:15.610 --> 01:14:19.610
a Link Between SIBO Probiotics
and Metabolic Acidosis".

01:14:19.610 --> 01:14:20.810
It was published in 2018.

01:14:20.810 --> 01:14:22.460
We can provide a link to this study.

01:14:22.460 --> 01:14:25.590
And there are several other
studies in the references

01:14:25.590 --> 01:14:29.930
that point to the fact that in some cases,

01:14:29.930 --> 01:14:32.870
excessive intake of probiotics
and excessive proliferation

01:14:32.870 --> 01:14:35.223
of gut microbiota can
actually be problematic.

01:14:36.120 --> 01:14:37.770
I mention this not to confuse you

01:14:37.770 --> 01:14:41.130
but because it is confusing out there.

01:14:41.130 --> 01:14:42.920
We all would think that just increasing

01:14:42.920 --> 01:14:45.790
microbiotal diversity
is always a good thing

01:14:45.790 --> 01:14:47.980
but there are thresholds

01:14:47.980 --> 01:14:50.810
beyond which excessive
microbiotal diversity

01:14:50.810 --> 01:14:52.050
might be problematic.

01:14:52.050 --> 01:14:54.120
I think everyone agrees that having

01:14:54.120 --> 01:14:59.120
too few microbial species
living in us is not a good idea.

01:14:59.480 --> 01:15:01.080
Now, none of that answers the questions

01:15:01.080 --> 01:15:03.970
that I think everyone
really wants answers to,

01:15:03.970 --> 01:15:06.450
which are, what should we do,

01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:09.310
what should we not do to
improve our gut microbiome?

01:15:09.310 --> 01:15:11.810
I mean, clearly we can't time travel back

01:15:11.810 --> 01:15:14.010
to when we were zero to three years old

01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:16.780
and get a dog if we didn't have a dog,

01:15:16.780 --> 01:15:18.920
get breastfed if we weren't breastfed,

01:15:18.920 --> 01:15:21.610
be delivered vaginally as
opposed to by C-section

01:15:21.610 --> 01:15:23.300
if we didn't have that opportunity.

01:15:23.300 --> 01:15:25.320
We just can't time travel and do that.

01:15:25.320 --> 01:15:27.850
All of us, however, should
be seeking to improve

01:15:27.850 --> 01:15:29.390
the conditions of our gut microbiome

01:15:29.390 --> 01:15:32.010
because of the critical
ways in which it impacts

01:15:32.010 --> 01:15:34.710
the rest of our brain and bodily health.

01:15:34.710 --> 01:15:36.520
So what should we do,
what shouldn't we do?

01:15:36.520 --> 01:15:39.060
Clearly we know that stress
can negatively impact

01:15:39.060 --> 01:15:40.123
the gut microbiome.

01:15:41.110 --> 01:15:44.040
However, some forms of
stress that can quote unquote

01:15:44.040 --> 01:15:45.690
negatively impact the microbiome

01:15:45.690 --> 01:15:48.090
include fasting, long periods of fasts,

01:15:48.090 --> 01:15:51.630
which makes sense because a
lot of microbiota need food

01:15:51.630 --> 01:15:52.900
in order to thrive.

01:15:52.900 --> 01:15:55.853
In fact, many if not all
of them do at some point.

01:15:57.550 --> 01:15:58.440
There are other questions

01:15:58.440 --> 01:16:01.010
such as should we eat particular foods

01:16:01.010 --> 01:16:02.990
and how often should we eat those foods?

01:16:02.990 --> 01:16:05.960
We've all been told that
fiber is incredibly important

01:16:05.960 --> 01:16:08.730
because of the presence
of prebiotic fiber,

01:16:08.730 --> 01:16:11.680
which can essentially feed the microbiome,

01:16:11.680 --> 01:16:15.050
but is fiber really necessary

01:16:15.050 --> 01:16:17.980
and how necessary is it to
encourage a healthy microbiome?

01:16:17.980 --> 01:16:19.400
Clearly, there are a number of people

01:16:19.400 --> 01:16:21.650
following relatively low fiber diets,

01:16:21.650 --> 01:16:22.930
such as ketogenic diets,

01:16:22.930 --> 01:16:25.310
and those can have, in some cases,

01:16:25.310 --> 01:16:27.530
anti-inflammatory
effects and can sometimes

01:16:28.380 --> 01:16:31.090
also improve certain microbiota species.

01:16:31.090 --> 01:16:32.470
So it can all be rather confusing.

01:16:32.470 --> 01:16:35.890
And for that matter, I
asked our resident expert,

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:38.850
Dr. Justin Sonnenburg at Stanford,

01:16:38.850 --> 01:16:39.820
all of these questions.

01:16:39.820 --> 01:16:41.880
And he answers them very systematically

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:45.030
in the episode that
comes out after this one.

01:16:45.030 --> 01:16:46.660
But I don't want to
withhold anything from you

01:16:46.660 --> 01:16:49.320
so I'll just give a
very top contour version

01:16:49.320 --> 01:16:52.550
of those answers and then
you'll get more in-depth answers

01:16:52.550 --> 01:16:54.170
during that episode.

01:16:54.170 --> 01:16:55.440
I asked about fasting.

01:16:55.440 --> 01:16:57.860
And the reason I asked about
fasting is that years ago,

01:16:57.860 --> 01:16:59.310
I was at a meeting as part

01:16:59.310 --> 01:17:02.150
of the Pew Biomedical Scholars Meeting

01:17:02.150 --> 01:17:05.600
and one of the other
Pew Biomedical Scholars

01:17:05.600 --> 01:17:07.210
was an expert in gut microbiome

01:17:07.210 --> 01:17:11.580
and I said, "Hey, are probiotics
good for the microbiome?

01:17:11.580 --> 01:17:14.230
And if so, which ones should I take?"

01:17:14.230 --> 01:17:15.510
And his answer was very interesting.

01:17:15.510 --> 01:17:18.500
He said, "You know, in
certain cases they can be,

01:17:18.500 --> 01:17:21.310
especially if you're
traveling or you're stressed,

01:17:21.310 --> 01:17:24.840
but it turns out that
the particular bacteria

01:17:24.840 --> 01:17:26.750
that they put in most probiotics

01:17:26.750 --> 01:17:30.780
don't actually replenish the
microbiota that you need most."

01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:32.980
And I thought, "Oh, well,
why don't they make ones

01:17:32.980 --> 01:17:35.670
that replenish the microbiota
that you need most?"

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:39.280
And his answer was, "Well,
they don't replenish those

01:17:39.280 --> 01:17:41.470
but they replenish other ones

01:17:41.470 --> 01:17:44.390
that then in turn
encourage the development

01:17:44.390 --> 01:17:46.160
of the microbiota that you do want

01:17:46.160 --> 01:17:47.960
once you start eating
the appropriate foods.

01:17:47.960 --> 01:17:49.320
So they change the environment

01:17:49.320 --> 01:17:50.520
which makes the environment better,

01:17:50.520 --> 01:17:53.340
which indirectly supports
the proliferation

01:17:53.340 --> 01:17:55.620
of quote unquote good microbiota."

01:17:55.620 --> 01:17:57.520
Okay, so that was a
somewhat convoluted answer

01:17:57.520 --> 01:17:59.020
but I did appreciate his answer.

01:17:59.020 --> 01:18:00.730
Then I asked him about fasting.

01:18:00.730 --> 01:18:02.860
I said, "Well, a lot of
people are getting interested

01:18:02.860 --> 01:18:04.790
in intermittent fasting now.

01:18:04.790 --> 01:18:06.940
People are spending a significant portion

01:18:06.940 --> 01:18:08.930
of each 24-hour cycle avoiding food

01:18:08.930 --> 01:18:10.370
for sake of time restrictive feeding.

01:18:10.370 --> 01:18:11.830
What does that do to the gut microbiome?

01:18:11.830 --> 01:18:14.510
Does it make it healthier or
does it make it unhealthier?"

01:18:14.510 --> 01:18:17.950
Well, my colleague from
Yale and Dr. Sonnenburg

01:18:17.950 --> 01:18:20.460
both confirmed that
during periods of fasting,

01:18:20.460 --> 01:18:22.440
especially prolonged periods of fasting,

01:18:22.440 --> 01:18:23.980
we actually start to digest away

01:18:23.980 --> 01:18:26.070
much of our digestive tract.

01:18:26.070 --> 01:18:28.040
Now, the whole thing
doesn't start to disappear

01:18:28.040 --> 01:18:29.910
but there's thinning of the mucosal lining

01:18:29.910 --> 01:18:31.860
or the least disruption
of the mucosal lining.

01:18:31.860 --> 01:18:35.370
A lot of the microbiota
species can start to die off.

01:18:35.370 --> 01:18:38.970
And so it was surprising to
me, but nonetheless interesting

01:18:38.970 --> 01:18:42.110
that fasting may actually
cause a disruption

01:18:42.110 --> 01:18:45.293
to certain healthy elements
of the gut microbiome.

01:18:46.150 --> 01:18:47.440
But again, there's a caveat.

01:18:47.440 --> 01:18:52.100
The caveat is that when people
eat after a period of fast,

01:18:52.100 --> 01:18:55.120
there may be a compensatory proliferation,

01:18:55.120 --> 01:18:58.280
meaning an increase in
healthy gut microbiota.

01:18:58.280 --> 01:18:59.450
So you start to get the picture

01:18:59.450 --> 01:19:02.100
that fasting is neither good nor bad.

01:19:02.100 --> 01:19:04.830
You start to get the picture
that particular diets,

01:19:04.830 --> 01:19:07.730
meaning certain restriction diets

01:19:07.730 --> 01:19:10.700
or macronutrient-rich diets
may not be good or bad

01:19:10.700 --> 01:19:11.780
for the microbiome.

01:19:11.780 --> 01:19:15.530
And yet there are some
answers that arrive to us

01:19:15.530 --> 01:19:18.460
from Dr. Sonnenburg, but from
other experts in the field,

01:19:18.460 --> 01:19:20.970
that there are certain foods

01:19:20.970 --> 01:19:22.610
and certain things that we can ingest

01:19:22.610 --> 01:19:25.500
which definitely enhance the microbiome

01:19:25.500 --> 01:19:27.650
and make it healthier than it would be

01:19:27.650 --> 01:19:30.120
were we to not ingest those foods.

01:19:30.120 --> 01:19:32.270
So next I'd like to
talk about what I think

01:19:32.270 --> 01:19:35.200
is a really pioneering and
important study in this area.

01:19:35.200 --> 01:19:38.700
This is a study that was carried
out by the Sonnenburg lab

01:19:38.700 --> 01:19:42.320
in collaboration with Chris
Gardner's lab, also at Stanford,

01:19:42.320 --> 01:19:47.150
where they compared two general
types of diets in humans,

01:19:47.150 --> 01:19:49.240
diets that were fiber rich,

01:19:49.240 --> 01:19:51.490
which has been proposed
time and time again

01:19:51.490 --> 01:19:54.340
to enhance microbiota diversity

01:19:54.340 --> 01:19:56.500
and to enhance gut-brain signaling even

01:19:56.500 --> 01:19:59.270
and to enhance the immune system perhaps,

01:19:59.270 --> 01:20:01.240
and diets that were enriched

01:20:01.240 --> 01:20:04.060
in so-called low-sugar fermented foods.

01:20:04.060 --> 01:20:06.870
Before I dive into that study
and what the conclusions were

01:20:06.870 --> 01:20:08.010
because they are very interesting

01:20:08.010 --> 01:20:10.120
and very actionable for all of us,

01:20:10.120 --> 01:20:12.360
I do want to touch on probiotics

01:20:12.360 --> 01:20:14.713
because I want to avoid confusion.

01:20:15.910 --> 01:20:18.510
It is not the case that
ingestion of probiotics

01:20:18.510 --> 01:20:20.200
will always lead to brain fog.

01:20:20.200 --> 01:20:21.970
I want to make that clear.

01:20:21.970 --> 01:20:24.760
It is the case that
ingestion of probiotics,

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:28.360
even if those probiotics
don't directly contain

01:20:28.360 --> 01:20:31.630
the microbiota species that
one is trying to proliferate,

01:20:31.630 --> 01:20:35.890
can be useful for improving
microbiotal diversity.

01:20:35.890 --> 01:20:38.940
In general, it seems that maintaining

01:20:38.940 --> 01:20:40.700
a healthy gut microbiome

01:20:40.700 --> 01:20:42.987
involves ingesting certain types of foods,

01:20:42.987 --> 01:20:45.560
and we'll talk about those in a moment,

01:20:45.560 --> 01:20:50.090
but perhaps also augmenting
the microbiota system

01:20:50.090 --> 01:20:53.710
through prebiotics or
probiotics at a fairly low level

01:20:53.710 --> 01:20:54.860
on a consistent basis.

01:20:54.860 --> 01:20:56.550
So these are not high dose probiotics

01:20:56.550 --> 01:21:00.320
except under conditions of dysbiosis

01:21:00.320 --> 01:21:02.060
where, for instance, if somebody has done

01:21:02.060 --> 01:21:05.540
a round of antibiotics
and they need to replenish

01:21:05.540 --> 01:21:07.090
their gut microbiome,

01:21:07.090 --> 01:21:09.410
there are foods and there are pill form

01:21:09.410 --> 01:21:11.980
and powder form prebiotics and probiotics

01:21:11.980 --> 01:21:13.180
that can be very useful.

01:21:14.190 --> 01:21:17.050
Or in cases where people
have been very stressed

01:21:17.050 --> 01:21:19.560
or are undergoing excessive travel

01:21:19.560 --> 01:21:22.580
or have shifted their diet radically,

01:21:22.580 --> 01:21:23.640
maybe that's due to travel,

01:21:23.640 --> 01:21:24.590
maybe that's due to illness,

01:21:24.590 --> 01:21:26.060
maybe that's due to stress.

01:21:26.060 --> 01:21:28.630
But when there are a number
of different converging events

01:21:28.630 --> 01:21:33.630
that are stressing or depleting
microbiotal diversity,

01:21:33.630 --> 01:21:36.290
that's when at least I
believe it can be useful

01:21:36.290 --> 01:21:37.960
to support the gut microbiome

01:21:37.960 --> 01:21:42.010
through the ingestion of quality
probiotics or prebiotics.

01:21:42.010 --> 01:21:45.390
So it would be under conditions
where people are stressed

01:21:45.390 --> 01:21:47.780
or their system is generally stressed

01:21:47.780 --> 01:21:50.730
for environmental or
illness-related reasons

01:21:50.730 --> 01:21:54.080
that it might be useful to
lean towards higher doses

01:21:54.080 --> 01:21:57.820
of prebiotics or probiotics
than one might normally use

01:21:57.820 --> 01:21:59.730
but that under normal conditions,

01:21:59.730 --> 01:22:03.240
that one would focus on quality nutrients

01:22:03.240 --> 01:22:07.567
through diet and focus on
ingestion of probiotics

01:22:08.490 --> 01:22:11.300
at a fairly low to moderate level,

01:22:11.300 --> 01:22:15.050
and/or prebiotics at a
fairly low to moderate level.

01:22:15.050 --> 01:22:16.770
That just seems like the logical approach

01:22:16.770 --> 01:22:19.000
based on the experts that I've spoken to.

01:22:19.000 --> 01:22:22.180
But certainly if your doctor prescribes

01:22:22.180 --> 01:22:24.610
or suggests that you take
high levels of probiotics

01:22:24.610 --> 01:22:25.640
for any reason,

01:22:25.640 --> 01:22:27.220
you should definitely pay
attention to your physician,

01:22:27.220 --> 01:22:29.250
and you should obviously pay
attention to your physician

01:22:29.250 --> 01:22:30.240
in any a case.

01:22:30.240 --> 01:22:32.410
You should never add or remove anything

01:22:32.410 --> 01:22:35.010
from your nutritional plan
or supplementation plan

01:22:35.010 --> 01:22:37.150
without consulting a physician.

01:22:37.150 --> 01:22:39.550
So what should we do in
order to maximize the health

01:22:39.550 --> 01:22:42.160
of our gut-brain axis as it's called?

01:22:42.160 --> 01:22:45.930
How should we support the
diversity of the good microbiota

01:22:45.930 --> 01:22:47.860
that help us create all
these neurotransmitters

01:22:47.860 --> 01:22:52.060
that we want, improve our
immune system function,

01:22:52.060 --> 01:22:53.910
and so on and so forth?

01:22:53.910 --> 01:22:57.740
Well, some of that is going
to be through the basics.

01:22:57.740 --> 01:22:58.620
When I say the basics,

01:22:58.620 --> 01:23:01.360
I mean the foundational
things that really set us up

01:23:01.360 --> 01:23:02.270
for overall health.

01:23:02.270 --> 01:23:05.280
So this is going to be getting deep sleep

01:23:05.280 --> 01:23:09.640
of sufficient duration 80
plus percent of the time.

01:23:09.640 --> 01:23:10.950
I mean, if you could get a
hundred percent of the time,

01:23:10.950 --> 01:23:12.980
that'd be great but very
few people accomplish that.

01:23:12.980 --> 01:23:14.250
It's going to be proper hydration.

01:23:14.250 --> 01:23:16.150
It's going to be proper
social interactions.

01:23:16.150 --> 01:23:17.730
It's going to be proper nutrition.

01:23:17.730 --> 01:23:19.260
And we'll talk more about
nutrition in a moment.

01:23:19.260 --> 01:23:20.670
It's going to be limiting

01:23:20.670 --> 01:23:24.240
excessive, prolonged stressors or stress.

01:23:24.240 --> 01:23:26.280
And indeed we've done episodes about,

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:27.610
just about all of those things

01:23:27.610 --> 01:23:29.030
but certainly about stress

01:23:29.030 --> 01:23:31.010
we have an episode of
the Huberman Lab Podcast

01:23:31.010 --> 01:23:32.560
that you can find at hubermanlab.com

01:23:32.560 --> 01:23:33.850
all about mastering stress,

01:23:33.850 --> 01:23:37.210
how to avoid long periods
of intense stress,

01:23:37.210 --> 01:23:38.663
what to do to offset those.

01:23:40.170 --> 01:23:43.450
Given that stress can
disrupt the microbiome,

01:23:43.450 --> 01:23:45.970
whether or not you're fasting or not,

01:23:45.970 --> 01:23:48.490
those tools ought to be useful.

01:23:48.490 --> 01:23:51.360
Now, in what I consider
to be a landmark study

01:23:51.360 --> 01:23:54.230
exploring the relationship
between the gut microbiome,

01:23:54.230 --> 01:23:58.060
food intake, and overall
health is this paper

01:23:58.060 --> 01:24:01.590
from Justin Sonnenburg's
lab and Chris Gardner's lab,

01:24:01.590 --> 01:24:03.650
both of which are at Stanford.

01:24:03.650 --> 01:24:06.530
And the paper entitled "Gut
Microbiome-Targeted Diets

01:24:06.530 --> 01:24:08.120
Modulate Human Immune Status"

01:24:08.120 --> 01:24:09.570
was published in the journal "Cell",

01:24:09.570 --> 01:24:12.807
which is among the three top
journals perhaps in the world,

01:24:12.807 --> 01:24:13.870
"Nature", "Science", and "Cell"

01:24:13.870 --> 01:24:17.543
really being the apex
journals for overall science,

01:24:18.700 --> 01:24:21.160
and especially for biomedical sciences.

01:24:21.160 --> 01:24:23.450
Now, this is a very interesting study.

01:24:23.450 --> 01:24:26.000
It was done on humans.
There were two major groups.

01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:29.100
One group of humans was
instructed to increase

01:24:29.100 --> 01:24:30.860
the amount of fiber in their diet

01:24:30.860 --> 01:24:33.410
and in fact ate a high fiber diet.

01:24:33.410 --> 01:24:35.630
The other group was instructed to eat

01:24:35.630 --> 01:24:38.690
a high fermented food diet.

01:24:38.690 --> 01:24:42.570
Now, both groups started
off not having eaten

01:24:42.570 --> 01:24:45.130
a lot of fiber or a lot of fermented foods

01:24:45.130 --> 01:24:47.830
and were told to increase
the amount of either fiber

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:49.750
or fermented foods that
they were ingesting

01:24:49.750 --> 01:24:51.630
over a four-week ramp up period

01:24:51.630 --> 01:24:54.690
and that was to avoid any
major gastric distress.

01:24:54.690 --> 01:24:56.750
It turns out that if you're
not already accustomed

01:24:56.750 --> 01:24:57.790
to eating a lot of fiber,

01:24:57.790 --> 01:25:00.190
increasing your amount
of fiber dramatically

01:25:00.190 --> 01:25:02.050
can cause some gastric distress

01:25:02.050 --> 01:25:04.680
but if you ease into it
over time, as we'll see,

01:25:04.680 --> 01:25:05.910
there's a mechanism behind this,

01:25:05.910 --> 01:25:08.070
which was unveiled in this study,

01:25:08.070 --> 01:25:09.660
but if you ease into it over time,

01:25:09.660 --> 01:25:11.600
then the system can tolerate it.

01:25:11.600 --> 01:25:13.310
Likewise high fermented foods

01:25:14.340 --> 01:25:16.180
can be readily tolerated

01:25:16.180 --> 01:25:18.680
if there's a ramp up phase of ingesting

01:25:18.680 --> 01:25:19.810
maybe one serving a day,

01:25:19.810 --> 01:25:23.220
then maybe two servings,
and ramping up in this case

01:25:23.220 --> 01:25:25.213
as high as six servings per day.

01:25:26.630 --> 01:25:29.970
However, after this ramp up period,

01:25:29.970 --> 01:25:32.090
the group assigned to
the high fiber condition

01:25:32.090 --> 01:25:34.940
maintained high fiber intake for six weeks

01:25:34.940 --> 01:25:37.330
and the high fermented
food group maintained

01:25:37.330 --> 01:25:40.410
high fermented food intake for six weeks

01:25:40.410 --> 01:25:42.820
after which they went off

01:25:42.820 --> 01:25:45.620
either the high fiber or
the high fermented food diet

01:25:45.620 --> 01:25:47.710
and there was a four-week follow up period

01:25:47.710 --> 01:25:50.470
during which they gradually
returned to baseline.

01:25:50.470 --> 01:25:53.650
Throughout the study their
gut microbiome was evaluated

01:25:53.650 --> 01:25:56.240
for the diversity of gut microbiota.

01:25:56.240 --> 01:25:58.170
And there were also a number of measures

01:25:58.170 --> 01:25:59.840
of immune system function,

01:25:59.840 --> 01:26:03.230
in particular measures of
the so-called inflammatome.

01:26:03.230 --> 01:26:05.550
The immune system has a lot of
different molecules involved.

01:26:05.550 --> 01:26:07.230
I did a whole episode
about the immune system

01:26:07.230 --> 01:26:08.260
if you're interested in learning

01:26:08.260 --> 01:26:09.870
what some of those molecules are,

01:26:09.870 --> 01:26:12.000
various cytokines and signaling molecules

01:26:12.000 --> 01:26:14.830
that reflect either
high inflammation states

01:26:14.830 --> 01:26:17.420
or reduced inflammation
states in the brain and body.

01:26:17.420 --> 01:26:19.130
You're welcome to check that episode.

01:26:19.130 --> 01:26:21.660
It's also at hubermanlab.com.

01:26:21.660 --> 01:26:24.040
Regardless, in this study,

01:26:24.040 --> 01:26:27.440
they explored the sorts of immune markers

01:26:27.440 --> 01:26:29.630
that were expressed in
either of the two groups

01:26:29.630 --> 01:26:30.900
and compared those.

01:26:30.900 --> 01:26:32.830
The basic takeaway of this paper

01:26:32.830 --> 01:26:36.293
was that contrary to what they predicted,

01:26:37.230 --> 01:26:39.160
the high fiber diet did not lead

01:26:39.160 --> 01:26:41.470
to increased microbiota diversity,

01:26:41.470 --> 01:26:43.050
at least not in all cases.

01:26:43.050 --> 01:26:45.550
And that was somewhat surprising.

01:26:45.550 --> 01:26:48.180
You know, the idea is that prebiotic fiber

01:26:48.180 --> 01:26:50.760
and a lot of the material
in fruits and vegetables

01:26:50.760 --> 01:26:51.980
and grains and so forth

01:26:52.960 --> 01:26:56.420
are supposed to support
microbiotal diversity

01:26:56.420 --> 01:26:59.380
and the proliferation
of existing microbiota.

01:26:59.380 --> 01:27:01.680
And that is not what they observed,

01:27:01.680 --> 01:27:03.640
although I want to be
very clear in pointing out

01:27:03.640 --> 01:27:06.840
that the results do
not indicate that fiber

01:27:06.840 --> 01:27:10.090
is not useful for health overall,

01:27:10.090 --> 01:27:13.070
but it does point to the fact
that increasing fiber intake

01:27:13.070 --> 01:27:15.150
did not increase microbiota diversity,

01:27:15.150 --> 01:27:17.600
which in general, as I mentioned before,

01:27:17.600 --> 01:27:21.970
is associated with improvements
in microbiota function,

01:27:21.970 --> 01:27:24.930
health, and overall wellbeing.

01:27:24.930 --> 01:27:27.000
Now, the high fermented
food diet condition

01:27:27.000 --> 01:27:28.490
was very interesting.

01:27:28.490 --> 01:27:31.570
It resulted in increased
microbiome diversity

01:27:31.570 --> 01:27:35.070
and decrease inflammatory
signals and activity.

01:27:35.070 --> 01:27:36.590
So there was a twofer.

01:27:36.590 --> 01:27:39.250
Basically by ingesting
high fermented foods

01:27:40.108 --> 01:27:41.620
in fair abundance, right?

01:27:41.620 --> 01:27:43.650
You know, four to six
servings or more per day

01:27:43.650 --> 01:27:46.140
is a lot of fermented food intake.

01:27:46.140 --> 01:27:48.160
We'll talk about what
some of those foods were.

01:27:48.160 --> 01:27:50.390
But the outcome was very positive.

01:27:50.390 --> 01:27:53.580
There was a clear increase
in microbiome diversity

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:56.070
and decreased inflammatory signals.

01:27:56.070 --> 01:27:58.800
So things like interleukin-6,

01:27:58.800 --> 01:28:00.750
a number of other
interleukins and cytokines

01:28:00.750 --> 01:28:03.550
that are associated with
increased inflammation

01:28:03.550 --> 01:28:06.450
in the brain and body were
reduced significantly.

01:28:06.450 --> 01:28:08.920
Now, let's talk a little
bit about this notion

01:28:08.920 --> 01:28:10.950
of number of servings, et cetera.

01:28:10.950 --> 01:28:14.010
One somewhat minor point of the study,

01:28:14.010 --> 01:28:15.180
but I think is useful in terms

01:28:15.180 --> 01:28:18.030
of taking an actionable stance with this

01:28:18.030 --> 01:28:21.280
is that the number of
servings of fermented foods

01:28:21.280 --> 01:28:25.440
was not as strong a
predictor of improvements

01:28:25.440 --> 01:28:28.330
in the inflammatome,
meaning reduced inflammation

01:28:28.330 --> 01:28:31.000
and improvements in microbiota diversity,

01:28:31.000 --> 01:28:34.790
as was the duration of
time that the individuals

01:28:34.790 --> 01:28:36.630
were ingesting fermented foods.

01:28:36.630 --> 01:28:38.020
In other words, the longer that one

01:28:38.020 --> 01:28:41.250
is consistently ingesting
fermented foods on a daily basis,

01:28:41.250 --> 01:28:44.190
the better the outcomes in
terms of the gut microbiome

01:28:44.190 --> 01:28:46.360
and for reducing inflammation.

01:28:46.360 --> 01:28:48.370
So I think that's an important point.

01:28:48.370 --> 01:28:49.700
And I make that point,

01:28:49.700 --> 01:28:52.050
especially because for a lot of people,

01:28:52.050 --> 01:28:53.380
even if you do this ramp up phase,

01:28:53.380 --> 01:28:55.680
six servings per day of fermented foods

01:28:55.680 --> 01:28:57.380
can seem like quite a lot.

01:28:57.380 --> 01:28:59.950
So what are these fermented foods?

01:28:59.950 --> 01:29:03.480
I think many of us are
familiar with certain cheeses

01:29:03.480 --> 01:29:05.820
and being fermented and
beer being fermented

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:07.610
and kombucha is fermented.

01:29:07.610 --> 01:29:09.320
In this study, they focus specifically

01:29:09.320 --> 01:29:11.590
on low-sugar fermented foods.

01:29:11.590 --> 01:29:13.263
So this would be plain yogurt,

01:29:14.680 --> 01:29:17.890
in some cases, kimchi or sauerkraut.

01:29:17.890 --> 01:29:19.570
An important consideration, however,

01:29:19.570 --> 01:29:21.400
is that it needs to contain

01:29:21.400 --> 01:29:23.170
what are called live active cultures,

01:29:23.170 --> 01:29:26.500
which means there actually
have to be microbiota

01:29:26.500 --> 01:29:28.510
that are alive inside the sauerkraut.

01:29:28.510 --> 01:29:30.280
One way you know whether
or not that's happening

01:29:30.280 --> 01:29:34.490
is if you purchase sauerkraut
or pickles or kimchi

01:29:34.490 --> 01:29:35.970
from a jar or a container

01:29:35.970 --> 01:29:38.980
that's on the non-refrigerated shelf

01:29:38.980 --> 01:29:41.600
or the non-refrigerated
section of your grocery store,

01:29:41.600 --> 01:29:43.430
it is not going to contain

01:29:43.430 --> 01:29:46.490
live active cultures of microbiota.

01:29:46.490 --> 01:29:49.470
And likewise, if you consume yogurt

01:29:49.470 --> 01:29:53.130
that has a lot of sugar or
other components added to it,

01:29:53.130 --> 01:29:55.410
it's not going to have
the same positive effect

01:29:55.410 --> 01:29:56.490
on the microbiome,

01:29:56.490 --> 01:29:57.980
at least that's the prediction

01:29:57.980 --> 01:29:59.190
given some of the relationship

01:29:59.190 --> 01:30:02.020
between the sorts of
microbiota that live in sugar

01:30:02.020 --> 01:30:04.210
versus plain type yogurts.

01:30:04.210 --> 01:30:06.030
They gave people the option of consuming

01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:08.810
any number of different
low-sugar fermented food.

01:30:08.810 --> 01:30:10.970
So that again that could
be sauerkraut, kimchi,

01:30:10.970 --> 01:30:13.113
things like kefir, natto.

01:30:13.950 --> 01:30:17.810
In Japan, they consume natto
which is a fermented food.

01:30:17.810 --> 01:30:20.383
Beer was not one of the fermented foods

01:30:20.383 --> 01:30:22.880
that was included in
the fermented food list.

01:30:22.880 --> 01:30:25.150
And when we say six servings per day,

01:30:25.150 --> 01:30:29.100
that is indeed six, six ounce servings,

01:30:29.100 --> 01:30:31.620
or six, four to six ounce servings.

01:30:31.620 --> 01:30:34.860
It was not six servings of
what's listed on the package.

01:30:34.860 --> 01:30:37.590
So again, that turns
out to be a fair amount

01:30:37.590 --> 01:30:38.423
of fermented foods.

01:30:38.423 --> 01:30:39.256
How should you gauge

01:30:39.256 --> 01:30:40.180
whether or not you're
getting enough of this?

01:30:40.180 --> 01:30:43.410
Well, if you decide to
take on this protocol

01:30:43.410 --> 01:30:44.910
of ingesting more fermented foods,

01:30:44.910 --> 01:30:47.190
which at least by my read of this study

01:30:47.190 --> 01:30:49.220
and some of the follow up
work that's being done,

01:30:49.220 --> 01:30:50.930
sounds like a terrific idea

01:30:50.930 --> 01:30:52.820
if you want to improve your gut microbiome

01:30:52.820 --> 01:30:55.580
for all the great reasons
that one would want to,

01:30:55.580 --> 01:30:59.540
brain-body health, reduced
inflammation, and on and on,

01:30:59.540 --> 01:31:01.740
well then you definitely want to focus

01:31:01.740 --> 01:31:04.460
on fermented foods that
you enjoy consuming.

01:31:04.460 --> 01:31:06.220
So for you if that's kefir,

01:31:06.220 --> 01:31:07.830
or for you that's plain yogurt,

01:31:07.830 --> 01:31:09.230
or for you that's sauerkraut,

01:31:09.230 --> 01:31:12.110
which happens to be my personal favorite,

01:31:12.110 --> 01:31:14.700
then you want to make sure
that it's going to be something

01:31:14.700 --> 01:31:17.810
that you are going to enjoy
ingesting quite a lot of

01:31:17.810 --> 01:31:20.420
and that you're going to
be okay with ingesting

01:31:20.420 --> 01:31:21.900
probably throughout the day.

01:31:21.900 --> 01:31:24.010
Now, people follow different
meal schedules, of course,

01:31:24.010 --> 01:31:26.130
but this does require not just eating

01:31:26.130 --> 01:31:30.020
all the fermented foods just
before bedtime or at one meal.

01:31:30.020 --> 01:31:31.570
I suppose you could do that,

01:31:31.570 --> 01:31:33.670
but in general, it's going
to work best in terms

01:31:33.670 --> 01:31:35.360
of limiting gastric distress

01:31:35.360 --> 01:31:37.530
by spreading it out throughout the day.

01:31:37.530 --> 01:31:39.060
I also want to mention brine.

01:31:39.060 --> 01:31:43.650
Brine is the liquid that
surrounds sauerkraut.

01:31:43.650 --> 01:31:46.870
It's that very salty fluid.

01:31:46.870 --> 01:31:49.750
And that contains a lot
of active live cultures.

01:31:49.750 --> 01:31:53.410
And they did include or they
allowed people to include

01:31:53.410 --> 01:31:55.800
brine in this study.

01:31:55.800 --> 01:31:57.910
And in discussions with Dr. Sonnenburg,

01:31:57.910 --> 01:32:01.020
which we'll go into in
more detail on the episode

01:32:01.020 --> 01:32:02.210
that comes out next week,

01:32:02.210 --> 01:32:04.190
we talk a lot about the particular value

01:32:04.190 --> 01:32:05.400
that brine might hold

01:32:05.400 --> 01:32:08.570
in terms of bringing
about microbiota diversity

01:32:08.570 --> 01:32:12.060
because of the richness of
live cultures that it contains.

01:32:12.060 --> 01:32:14.540
I do want to focus for a moment
on the high fiber condition

01:32:14.540 --> 01:32:16.210
because there were some
interesting observations

01:32:16.210 --> 01:32:18.810
about the people that were
placed into that condition.

01:32:18.810 --> 01:32:23.170
First of all, increasing
the amount of fiber

01:32:23.170 --> 01:32:26.990
definitely increased the number of enzymes

01:32:26.990 --> 01:32:29.840
that can be used to digest fiber.

01:32:29.840 --> 01:32:32.880
This is in keeping with this
idea of this ramp up phase

01:32:32.880 --> 01:32:35.120
where accumulation of more fiber intake

01:32:35.120 --> 01:32:37.680
can over time lead to
less gastric distress

01:32:37.680 --> 01:32:39.590
but also to more utilization of fiber

01:32:39.590 --> 01:32:41.330
which overall should be a good thing.

01:32:41.330 --> 01:32:43.830
So while they didn't observe an increase

01:32:43.830 --> 01:32:45.560
in immune system function

01:32:45.560 --> 01:32:47.720
or an increase in microbiota diversity,

01:32:47.720 --> 01:32:51.610
there was an increase in
these fiber digesting enzymes.

01:32:51.610 --> 01:32:53.090
They also observed what they called

01:32:53.090 --> 01:32:55.200
personalized immune responses.

01:32:55.200 --> 01:32:58.030
There were some subgroups
within the high fiber group

01:32:58.030 --> 01:33:00.760
that had interesting changes in terms

01:33:00.760 --> 01:33:05.120
of their reactions to, or I
should say their inflammatome,

01:33:05.120 --> 01:33:06.900
meaning the inflammatory
markers they expressed,

01:33:06.900 --> 01:33:09.710
as well as their microbiota diversity.

01:33:09.710 --> 01:33:11.100
So there were essentially three groups.

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:12.970
One group actually showed an increase

01:33:12.970 --> 01:33:14.620
in inflammatory markers.

01:33:14.620 --> 01:33:15.870
So that was quite surprising

01:33:15.870 --> 01:33:19.620
and probably not wonderful for the message

01:33:19.620 --> 01:33:21.200
that fiber is always good for us

01:33:21.200 --> 01:33:25.840
but that was a small cohort
within the fiber intake group.

01:33:25.840 --> 01:33:28.710
Another group and still another group,

01:33:28.710 --> 01:33:31.920
both showed reductions in
baseline microbiota diversity

01:33:31.920 --> 01:33:34.320
although to varying degrees.

01:33:34.320 --> 01:33:36.710
So I don't want to paint the
picture that fiber is bad

01:33:36.710 --> 01:33:39.120
but fiber certainly did not
have the positive effects

01:33:39.120 --> 01:33:40.830
on microbiota diversity

01:33:40.830 --> 01:33:43.220
that the high fermented food diet did.

01:33:43.220 --> 01:33:44.380
So my read of this study,

01:33:44.380 --> 01:33:47.000
and I think the stance
that many others have taken

01:33:47.000 --> 01:33:48.653
as a consequence of these data,

01:33:49.580 --> 01:33:52.600
is that we should be increasing
our fermented food intake,

01:33:52.600 --> 01:33:55.550
that that's simply a good
thing to do in order to support

01:33:55.550 --> 01:33:58.700
our gut microbiome and to
reduce inflammatory signals

01:33:58.700 --> 01:34:00.600
in our brain and body.

01:34:00.600 --> 01:34:02.530
And there are a number of
different ways to do that.

01:34:02.530 --> 01:34:04.270
I mentioned some of the particular foods.

01:34:04.270 --> 01:34:05.380
However, anytime you're talking

01:34:05.380 --> 01:34:07.460
about ingesting fermented foods,

01:34:07.460 --> 01:34:09.380
especially the high quality ones

01:34:09.380 --> 01:34:10.960
that come from the refrigerated section

01:34:10.960 --> 01:34:12.143
of the grocery store,

01:34:13.560 --> 01:34:16.470
and that have low sugar
content, et cetera,

01:34:16.470 --> 01:34:18.400
we do have to be considerate of cost

01:34:18.400 --> 01:34:20.440
because certain things like
kombuchas, for instance,

01:34:20.440 --> 01:34:22.550
can be quite costly.

01:34:22.550 --> 01:34:23.840
I should also mention some kombuchas

01:34:23.840 --> 01:34:26.730
actually contain alcohol, some do not,

01:34:26.730 --> 01:34:28.830
or contain very little amounts of alcohol.

01:34:30.100 --> 01:34:32.840
One way to avoid the high
cost of fermented foods

01:34:32.840 --> 01:34:34.600
while still being able to accumulate

01:34:34.600 --> 01:34:35.950
a lot of fermented food intake

01:34:35.950 --> 01:34:38.240
is to simply make those
fermented foods yourself.

01:34:38.240 --> 01:34:40.550
And this is something that
we've started exploring

01:34:40.550 --> 01:34:42.850
and experimenting with in our home.

01:34:42.850 --> 01:34:43.850
One simple way to do this

01:34:43.850 --> 01:34:45.630
is to just make your own sauerkraut.

01:34:45.630 --> 01:34:47.020
It involves very few ingredients.

01:34:47.020 --> 01:34:50.030
It basically involves
cabbage, water, and salt,

01:34:50.030 --> 01:34:52.150
but there's a specific process
that you need to follow

01:34:52.150 --> 01:34:56.230
in order to create these large
volumes of sauerkraut at home

01:34:56.230 --> 01:34:58.380
using that low cost method.

01:34:58.380 --> 01:35:00.610
The best resource that I know of

01:35:00.610 --> 01:35:03.120
in order to follow a great recipe

01:35:03.120 --> 01:35:06.160
to make homemade sauerkraut
would be the recipe

01:35:06.160 --> 01:35:07.780
for homemade sauerkraut that's contained

01:35:07.780 --> 01:35:09.850
in Tim Ferriss's book, "The 4-Hour Chef".

01:35:09.850 --> 01:35:11.580
There's an excellent protocol there.

01:35:11.580 --> 01:35:13.900
It involves chopping up the
cabbage, putting into a bowl,

01:35:13.900 --> 01:35:16.530
mashing it up with your hands,

01:35:16.530 --> 01:35:18.000
which can be fun,

01:35:18.000 --> 01:35:19.970
putting water in there,
some salt, covering it,

01:35:19.970 --> 01:35:22.010
and then keeping it in a
particular environment,

01:35:22.010 --> 01:35:23.850
and then routinely scraping off

01:35:23.850 --> 01:35:25.530
some of the material from the surface.

01:35:25.530 --> 01:35:27.810
You have to do that in order to make sure

01:35:27.810 --> 01:35:31.780
that you're not getting
microbes and things

01:35:31.780 --> 01:35:33.170
growing in it that are bad for you.

01:35:33.170 --> 01:35:34.820
So you definitely want
to pay careful attention

01:35:34.820 --> 01:35:37.630
to the protocol, but that's
a very, very low cost way

01:35:37.630 --> 01:35:40.050
of generating lots and
lots of fermented foods

01:35:40.050 --> 01:35:43.130
so you don't go broke trying
to improve your microbiome.

01:35:43.130 --> 01:35:44.090
The other thing that you can do

01:35:44.090 --> 01:35:46.530
if you're really obsessed with kombucha

01:35:46.530 --> 01:35:48.840
or something like that, to
avoid the high cost of kombucha

01:35:48.840 --> 01:35:51.070
is there are ways that
you can get the scoby,

01:35:51.070 --> 01:35:52.300
which basically allows you to make

01:35:52.300 --> 01:35:53.380
your own kombucha at home.

01:35:53.380 --> 01:35:55.850
I've never tried this,
but when I was a post doc,

01:35:55.850 --> 01:35:57.240
there was an undergraduate in the lab,

01:35:57.240 --> 01:35:59.770
I think, well, I won't out him,

01:35:59.770 --> 01:36:02.310
but he's now gone on to medical school

01:36:02.310 --> 01:36:04.500
and I think he's passed his residency

01:36:04.500 --> 01:36:06.360
and is a practicing doctor,

01:36:06.360 --> 01:36:08.800
but nonetheless, he was always
making kombucha at home.

01:36:08.800 --> 01:36:10.900
He told me it was exceedingly
easy, but then again,

01:36:10.900 --> 01:36:12.660
he had a number of other
skills and attributes

01:36:12.660 --> 01:36:14.560
that made me think that he could do

01:36:14.560 --> 01:36:15.890
pretty much anything with ease,

01:36:15.890 --> 01:36:18.470
whereas I tend to struggle
with even basic cooking.

01:36:18.470 --> 01:36:20.790
So maybe if you're feeling
a little more adventurous,

01:36:20.790 --> 01:36:23.360
you could explore making
your own kombucha.

01:36:23.360 --> 01:36:25.340
But there are a number of
different protocols and recipes

01:36:25.340 --> 01:36:28.400
out there for making your own
low-sugar fermented foods.

01:36:28.400 --> 01:36:31.460
So you needn't run out
and buy fresh sauerkraut

01:36:31.460 --> 01:36:32.293
all the time.

01:36:32.293 --> 01:36:34.530
I should also mention for those
of you that are interested

01:36:34.530 --> 01:36:38.700
in getting your fermented
intake from pickles,

01:36:38.700 --> 01:36:42.700
jarred pickles rarely
if ever contain ferment.

01:36:42.700 --> 01:36:45.220
Mostly they're just
soaked in vinegar, water,

01:36:45.220 --> 01:36:46.100
and with some spices,

01:36:46.100 --> 01:36:48.180
but there are some that contain ferment.

01:36:48.180 --> 01:36:50.730
You actually have to look
for that on the container.

01:36:50.730 --> 01:36:51.930
And I don't know, maybe someone out there

01:36:51.930 --> 01:36:54.700
knows how to make natto and
knows how to make kimchi well

01:36:54.700 --> 01:36:56.220
and things of that sort.

01:36:56.220 --> 01:36:59.150
It certainly is the case based
on the data from the study

01:36:59.150 --> 01:37:02.240
that ingesting more servings
of fermented food per day

01:37:02.240 --> 01:37:04.290
ought to be beneficial
for our gut microbiome.

01:37:04.290 --> 01:37:06.730
And since this is an episode
not just about gut microbiome

01:37:06.730 --> 01:37:08.520
but gut-brain health,

01:37:08.520 --> 01:37:11.020
I should mention that
one form of signaling

01:37:11.020 --> 01:37:12.730
between the gut microbiome and the brain

01:37:12.730 --> 01:37:14.050
which we did not discuss

01:37:14.050 --> 01:37:16.020
and I'll just touch on briefly

01:37:16.020 --> 01:37:21.020
is that when the inflammatome
or the genes and markers

01:37:21.030 --> 01:37:25.260
of inflammation are
kept in a healthy range,

01:37:25.260 --> 01:37:28.770
there's an active signaling
of that immune system status

01:37:28.770 --> 01:37:29.603
to the brain.

01:37:29.603 --> 01:37:32.620
There's an intermediate
cell type that communicates

01:37:32.620 --> 01:37:34.340
immune status to the brain.

01:37:34.340 --> 01:37:36.290
And that cell type is the microglial cell.

01:37:36.290 --> 01:37:38.730
It's a type of glia as the name suggests.

01:37:38.730 --> 01:37:41.540
When there's a lot of
inflammation in the body,

01:37:41.540 --> 01:37:43.810
these microglia actually get activated

01:37:43.810 --> 01:37:47.230
and can start eating away
at various components

01:37:47.230 --> 01:37:48.470
of the brain and nervous system.

01:37:48.470 --> 01:37:49.790
And I don't mean massive eating away.

01:37:49.790 --> 01:37:51.280
They're not going to
digest the whole brain

01:37:51.280 --> 01:37:55.260
but these microglia are sort
of the resident macrophages

01:37:55.260 --> 01:37:56.093
of the brain.

01:37:56.093 --> 01:37:58.730
Macrophages are in the periphery
and they gobble up debris

01:37:58.730 --> 01:37:59.840
and things of that sort.

01:37:59.840 --> 01:38:03.420
The microglia on a regular
basis are eating up debris

01:38:03.420 --> 01:38:05.880
that accumulates across waking cycles

01:38:05.880 --> 01:38:08.200
and in response to
micro-damage to the brain

01:38:08.200 --> 01:38:09.480
that occurs on a daily basis.

01:38:09.480 --> 01:38:11.850
So they have a lot of
important basic everyday

01:38:11.850 --> 01:38:13.760
what we call housekeeping functions.

01:38:13.760 --> 01:38:15.960
But when there's a lot of
inflammation in the body,

01:38:15.960 --> 01:38:18.730
when there's a massive immune response,

01:38:18.730 --> 01:38:20.850
the microglia can be hyperactivated

01:38:20.850 --> 01:38:22.470
and that's thought to lead to any number

01:38:22.470 --> 01:38:25.730
of different cognitive defects
or challenges thinking,

01:38:25.730 --> 01:38:29.220
or maybe even some forms of
neurodegeneration over time,

01:38:29.220 --> 01:38:31.480
although that last point
is more of a hypothesis

01:38:31.480 --> 01:38:34.100
than a well tamped down
fact at this point.

01:38:34.100 --> 01:38:36.730
There's still a lot of
investigation to be done in humans.

01:38:36.730 --> 01:38:39.600
The animal data, however,
are very, very strong

01:38:39.600 --> 01:38:41.530
that when the immune system is activated

01:38:41.530 --> 01:38:44.170
or chronically activated or hyperactivated

01:38:44.170 --> 01:38:45.860
that neural tissue, meaning brain tissue

01:38:45.860 --> 01:38:48.020
and other central nervous
system tissue can suffer.

01:38:48.020 --> 01:38:50.220
So there are a lot of reasons to want

01:38:50.220 --> 01:38:53.230
to not just improve microbiome diversity,

01:38:53.230 --> 01:38:56.200
but to also improve immune system function

01:38:56.200 --> 01:38:58.840
and to limit the number
of inflammatory markers

01:38:58.840 --> 01:39:00.160
that are present in the body

01:39:00.160 --> 01:39:02.360
because of the way those
inflammatory markers

01:39:02.360 --> 01:39:05.380
can signal deleterious
events in the brain.

01:39:05.380 --> 01:39:07.890
And while eating fermented foods

01:39:07.890 --> 01:39:09.880
and making your own fermented foods

01:39:09.880 --> 01:39:11.910
and buying high quality fermented foods

01:39:11.910 --> 01:39:13.790
might seem like an inconvenience,

01:39:13.790 --> 01:39:16.260
I would say that from the perspective

01:39:16.260 --> 01:39:18.800
of cost-benefit or effort-benefit,

01:39:18.800 --> 01:39:21.080
it's actually quite a good situation

01:39:21.080 --> 01:39:24.230
where if you can just ramp up
the number of fermented foods

01:39:24.230 --> 01:39:26.410
or servings of fermented foods
that you're eating per day

01:39:26.410 --> 01:39:27.730
over a period of a few weeks

01:39:27.730 --> 01:39:30.250
so that you're tolerating that well,

01:39:30.250 --> 01:39:32.270
that ought to have a very positive effect

01:39:32.270 --> 01:39:34.230
on your microbiome diversity

01:39:34.230 --> 01:39:36.760
and indeed on gut-brain function.

01:39:36.760 --> 01:39:38.150
And I'll be the last to suggest

01:39:38.150 --> 01:39:40.540
that people completely forego on fiber.

01:39:40.540 --> 01:39:41.890
I think there's some debate out there

01:39:41.890 --> 01:39:44.950
as to how much fiber we
need and whether or not

01:39:44.950 --> 01:39:47.290
certain forms of fiber
are better than others.

01:39:47.290 --> 01:39:48.660
I'm not going to get into that debate.

01:39:48.660 --> 01:39:51.690
It's barbed wire enough without
me injecting my own views

01:39:51.690 --> 01:39:52.800
into that debate.

01:39:52.800 --> 01:39:55.100
But I think there's ample
evidence to support the fact

01:39:55.100 --> 01:39:58.700
that for most people ingesting
a fair amount of fiber

01:39:58.700 --> 01:40:00.410
is going to be a good idea.

01:40:00.410 --> 01:40:01.243
I would just say that

01:40:01.243 --> 01:40:02.940
make sure that you're also
ingesting a fair amount

01:40:02.940 --> 01:40:04.200
of fermented foods.

01:40:04.200 --> 01:40:06.140
And along the lines of fiber,

01:40:06.140 --> 01:40:08.710
in an accompanying article
published in "Cell",

01:40:08.710 --> 01:40:11.710
which was sort of a what we
call a news and views piece

01:40:11.710 --> 01:40:15.350
about the Sonnenburg and Gardner paper,

01:40:15.350 --> 01:40:16.920
they make a quite good point,

01:40:16.920 --> 01:40:19.650
which is that the increase in fiber intake

01:40:19.650 --> 01:40:23.150
that led to the increase in
carbohydrate active enzymes,

01:40:23.150 --> 01:40:25.570
these CAZymes as they're called,

01:40:25.570 --> 01:40:28.300
these are enzymes that help digest fiber,

01:40:28.300 --> 01:40:31.860
quote, "indicating an enhanced
capacity for the microbiome

01:40:31.860 --> 01:40:35.430
to degrade complex carbohydrates
present in fibrous foods".

01:40:35.430 --> 01:40:40.430
So in other words, eating
more fiber and fibrous foods

01:40:40.920 --> 01:40:42.560
allowed for an increase in these enzymes

01:40:42.560 --> 01:40:44.880
that allow you to eat
still more fibrous foods

01:40:44.880 --> 01:40:47.680
or to better digest fibrous
foods that are coming in

01:40:47.680 --> 01:40:48.800
through other sources.

01:40:48.800 --> 01:40:52.610
So there is at least one
utility for increasing fiber

01:40:52.610 --> 01:40:55.590
even though it's separate from
the gut microbiotal diversity

01:40:55.590 --> 01:40:57.430
and reducing inflammation.

01:40:57.430 --> 01:40:59.870
And I'd be remiss if I didn't
touch on some of the data

01:40:59.870 --> 01:41:02.490
and controversy about
artificial sweeteners

01:41:02.490 --> 01:41:04.470
and the gut microbiome.

01:41:04.470 --> 01:41:06.830
I want to be very clear that
what I'm about to tell you

01:41:06.830 --> 01:41:09.300
has only been established
in animal models,

01:41:09.300 --> 01:41:11.820
in a mouse model, at
least to my knowledge.

01:41:11.820 --> 01:41:14.590
What the studies have shown,
and there were several,

01:41:14.590 --> 01:41:16.120
but one published in the journal "Nature"

01:41:16.120 --> 01:41:17.100
a few years back is the one

01:41:17.100 --> 01:41:18.730
that got the most amount of attention

01:41:18.730 --> 01:41:20.770
is that animals that consume large amounts

01:41:20.770 --> 01:41:22.250
of artificial sweeteners.

01:41:22.250 --> 01:41:25.060
in particular things like
saccharin or sucralose

01:41:25.060 --> 01:41:27.670
show disruptions in their gut microbiome.

01:41:27.670 --> 01:41:29.410
I'm not aware of any studies in humans

01:41:29.410 --> 01:41:30.730
that show the equivalent effect.

01:41:30.730 --> 01:41:33.330
And I'm not aware of any
studies in humans that show

01:41:33.330 --> 01:41:35.620
the equivalent effect for things

01:41:35.620 --> 01:41:37.840
like plant-based, low-calorie sweeteners,

01:41:37.840 --> 01:41:41.090
things like Stevia, monk
fruit, and things of that sort.

01:41:41.090 --> 01:41:43.420
And at least by my exploration,

01:41:43.420 --> 01:41:45.090
I couldn't find any data specifically

01:41:45.090 --> 01:41:47.790
related to the sweetener aspartame.

01:41:47.790 --> 01:41:50.380
So right now it's somewhat controversial

01:41:50.380 --> 01:41:52.880
and actually this is kind of
a third rail topic out there.

01:41:52.880 --> 01:41:54.820
When one group will come out saying

01:41:54.820 --> 01:41:56.100
that artificial sweeteners are bad

01:41:56.100 --> 01:41:58.110
because they disrupt the gut microbiome,

01:41:58.110 --> 01:42:01.400
the response generally from
a number of people as well,

01:42:01.400 --> 01:42:02.900
that's only been shown in animal models.

01:42:02.900 --> 01:42:04.310
And indeed that's true.

01:42:04.310 --> 01:42:06.840
So right now I don't think
that there's a strong case

01:42:06.840 --> 01:42:08.130
one way or the other.

01:42:08.130 --> 01:42:10.260
I think that people should
basically ask themselves

01:42:10.260 --> 01:42:13.130
whether or not they like
artificial sweeteners or not,

01:42:13.130 --> 01:42:15.570
whether or not they're
willing to risk it or not,

01:42:15.570 --> 01:42:18.110
and obviously that's an individual choice.

01:42:18.110 --> 01:42:20.680
I also want to point out a recent study

01:42:20.680 --> 01:42:23.020
from Diego Bohorquez's lab,

01:42:23.020 --> 01:42:26.880
which actually shows, however,
that neurons in the gut,

01:42:26.880 --> 01:42:30.730
those neuropod cells, are
actually capable of distinguishing

01:42:30.730 --> 01:42:33.830
between real sugars and
artificial sweeteners.

01:42:33.830 --> 01:42:36.270
This is a really interesting body of work.

01:42:36.270 --> 01:42:38.880
It was published just now, just recently,

01:42:38.880 --> 01:42:40.640
I should say, February 2022.

01:42:40.640 --> 01:42:41.757
The title of the paper is

01:42:41.757 --> 01:42:44.150
"The Preference for Sugar
over Sweetener Depends

01:42:44.150 --> 01:42:45.660
on a Gut Sensor Cell".

01:42:45.660 --> 01:42:47.200
And to make a long story short,

01:42:47.200 --> 01:42:50.360
what they showed was there's
a category of neuropod cells

01:42:50.360 --> 01:42:55.070
that recognize sugar in the
gut and signal that information

01:42:55.070 --> 01:42:58.070
about the presence of sugar
in the gut to the brain

01:42:58.070 --> 01:42:59.860
via the pathways we talked about before,

01:42:59.860 --> 01:43:01.660
the nodose ganglia, the vagus, dopamine,

01:43:01.660 --> 01:43:02.710
et cetera, et cetera.

01:43:03.610 --> 01:43:06.810
Interestingly, the very
same category of neurons

01:43:06.810 --> 01:43:10.270
can respond to artificial sweeteners

01:43:10.270 --> 01:43:12.140
and signal that information to the brain,

01:43:12.140 --> 01:43:14.760
but the pattern of signaling

01:43:14.760 --> 01:43:17.670
and indeed the signature pattern

01:43:17.670 --> 01:43:20.680
that is conveyed to the brain
and received by the brain

01:43:20.680 --> 01:43:22.760
is actually quite a bit different

01:43:22.760 --> 01:43:25.830
when these same neurons are responding

01:43:25.830 --> 01:43:29.730
to artificial sweeteners
versus actual sugar.

01:43:29.730 --> 01:43:31.277
And this is very interesting
because what it means

01:43:31.277 --> 01:43:34.500
is first of all that neurons

01:43:34.500 --> 01:43:36.180
have incredible specificity

01:43:36.180 --> 01:43:38.300
in terms of what they are
signaling from the gut

01:43:38.300 --> 01:43:39.480
to the brain.

01:43:39.480 --> 01:43:42.900
And it also means that there
may be a particular signal

01:43:42.900 --> 01:43:44.530
that the brain receives that says

01:43:44.530 --> 01:43:48.480
I'm receiving some intake of food or drink

01:43:48.480 --> 01:43:52.840
that tastes sweet but doesn't
actually offer much nutrients

01:43:52.840 --> 01:43:54.010
in the direction of sweetness,

01:43:54.010 --> 01:43:56.740
meaning that it doesn't have
calories despite being sweet.

01:43:56.740 --> 01:43:59.090
Now, again, this is all
subconscious processing.

01:43:59.090 --> 01:44:00.900
And like with the previous studies

01:44:00.900 --> 01:44:03.480
we were just discussing
about artificial sweeteners

01:44:03.480 --> 01:44:05.640
generally and the gut
microbiome generally,

01:44:05.640 --> 01:44:08.970
it's unclear how this relates to humans

01:44:08.970 --> 01:44:10.220
at this point in time.

01:44:10.220 --> 01:44:12.690
But given the similarity
of cellular processes

01:44:12.690 --> 01:44:15.890
and molecular processes at the
level of gut-brain in mice,

01:44:15.890 --> 01:44:18.430
I think it stands to reason
that these neuropod cells

01:44:18.430 --> 01:44:21.910
very likely are capable of
signaling sweet presence

01:44:21.910 --> 01:44:23.740
of real sweetener versus
artificial sweetener

01:44:23.740 --> 01:44:24.960
in humans as well,

01:44:24.960 --> 01:44:27.660
although that still remains
to be determined empirically.

01:44:27.660 --> 01:44:30.680
So I'd like to just briefly
recap what I've covered today.

01:44:30.680 --> 01:44:33.380
I started off by talking about
the structure and function

01:44:33.380 --> 01:44:35.110
of the gut-brain axis.

01:44:35.110 --> 01:44:37.580
I described the basic
structure and function

01:44:37.580 --> 01:44:39.550
of the digestive pathway,

01:44:39.550 --> 01:44:42.860
and how that digestive pathway
harbors microbiotal species,

01:44:42.860 --> 01:44:46.920
meaning many, many little
bacteria that can signal

01:44:46.920 --> 01:44:49.900
all sorts of things to the
rest of the brain and body.

01:44:49.900 --> 01:44:52.500
And indeed, we talked about
the various ways they do that.

01:44:52.500 --> 01:44:54.500
We talked about direct pathways,

01:44:54.500 --> 01:44:57.430
literally nerve networks
that extend from the gut

01:44:57.430 --> 01:45:00.430
up to the brain and from
the brain back to the gut.

01:45:00.430 --> 01:45:02.920
And we talked about indirect pathways,

01:45:02.920 --> 01:45:06.110
how some of the gut microbiota
can actually synthesize

01:45:06.110 --> 01:45:08.530
neurotransmitters that get
out into the bloodstream,

01:45:08.530 --> 01:45:09.650
can impact the body,

01:45:09.650 --> 01:45:11.470
can impact the immune system,

01:45:11.470 --> 01:45:14.810
and can get into the brain
and act as neurotransmitters

01:45:14.810 --> 01:45:17.470
in the brain just as
would neurotransmitters

01:45:17.470 --> 01:45:19.543
that originate from within the brain.

01:45:20.921 --> 01:45:22.740
We also talked about what constitutes

01:45:22.740 --> 01:45:25.400
a healthy versus unhealthy microbiome.

01:45:25.400 --> 01:45:28.440
And it's very clear that
having a diverse microbiome

01:45:28.440 --> 01:45:32.840
is healthier than having
a non-diverse microbiome.

01:45:32.840 --> 01:45:34.660
But as I pointed out,

01:45:34.660 --> 01:45:36.670
there's still a lot of
questions as to exactly

01:45:36.670 --> 01:45:38.900
what microbiota species
you want to enhance

01:45:38.900 --> 01:45:41.330
and which ones you want
to suppress in the gut

01:45:41.330 --> 01:45:44.513
in order to achieve the best
gut-brain axis function.

01:45:45.350 --> 01:45:47.750
We talked about how things
like fasting might impact

01:45:47.750 --> 01:45:49.810
the microbiome and how
some of that might be

01:45:49.810 --> 01:45:51.210
a little bit counterintuitive

01:45:51.210 --> 01:45:53.981
based on some of the other
positive effects of fasting,

01:45:53.981 --> 01:45:56.210
or if we're not just discussing fasting,

01:45:56.210 --> 01:45:58.870
some other types of
somewhat restrictive diets

01:45:58.870 --> 01:46:00.750
either restrictive in time or restrictive

01:46:00.750 --> 01:46:02.950
in terms of macronutrient intake,

01:46:02.950 --> 01:46:05.570
how those may or may
not improve the health

01:46:05.570 --> 01:46:06.630
of gut microbiome.

01:46:06.630 --> 01:46:09.580
And the basic takeaway was
that because we don't know

01:46:09.580 --> 01:46:13.070
exactly how specific diets
impact the gut microbiome,

01:46:13.070 --> 01:46:15.090
and we don't know how fasting

01:46:15.090 --> 01:46:17.860
either promotes or
degrades the microbiome,

01:46:17.860 --> 01:46:20.500
we really can't say whether
or not they are improving

01:46:20.500 --> 01:46:22.960
or degrading the microbiome at this time.

01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:25.910
However, it is clear that stress,

01:46:25.910 --> 01:46:27.140
in particular chronic stress,

01:46:27.140 --> 01:46:28.830
can disrupt the gut microbiome.

01:46:28.830 --> 01:46:29.810
It's also clear, of course,

01:46:29.810 --> 01:46:32.470
that antibiotics can
disrupt the gut microbiome.

01:46:32.470 --> 01:46:33.780
And that brings us to the topic

01:46:33.780 --> 01:46:35.820
of prebiotics and probiotics.

01:46:35.820 --> 01:46:38.420
And I emphasized the fact
that for most people,

01:46:38.420 --> 01:46:42.030
ingesting high quality non-processed foods

01:46:42.030 --> 01:46:44.720
that includes some prebiotic fiber

01:46:44.720 --> 01:46:47.390
but also that includes some probiotics

01:46:47.390 --> 01:46:48.620
will probably be healthy

01:46:48.620 --> 01:46:50.900
but not excessive levels of probiotics.

01:46:50.900 --> 01:46:52.860
High levels of supplemented probiotics

01:46:52.860 --> 01:46:54.570
of the sort that would
come in a probiotic pill

01:46:54.570 --> 01:46:56.540
or even prescription probiotics

01:46:56.540 --> 01:46:58.920
would probably lend
themselves best to when people

01:46:58.920 --> 01:47:01.060
were under severe chronic stress

01:47:01.060 --> 01:47:03.300
or had just come off a serious round

01:47:03.300 --> 01:47:06.780
or an ongoing or repeated
rounds of antibiotics.

01:47:06.780 --> 01:47:09.430
That does not mean that
ingesting probiotics

01:47:09.430 --> 01:47:11.550
in any form or any kind is not good.

01:47:11.550 --> 01:47:14.990
It just means that the
very high dose probiotics,

01:47:14.990 --> 01:47:16.980
again, typically found
in prescription form

01:47:16.980 --> 01:47:20.150
or capsule pill form
probably are best reserved

01:47:20.150 --> 01:47:22.860
to cases where of course
your doctor prescribes them.

01:47:22.860 --> 01:47:24.760
You should always follow
your doctor's advice.

01:47:24.760 --> 01:47:27.300
But in cases where
perhaps you are jetlagged,

01:47:27.300 --> 01:47:29.800
you're traveling
excessively for any reason,

01:47:29.800 --> 01:47:30.830
or working excessively,

01:47:30.830 --> 01:47:32.280
you're not getting enough sleep,

01:47:32.280 --> 01:47:35.240
or your diet is radically
changed from normal.

01:47:35.240 --> 01:47:38.260
And we talked about how
increasing the amount of fiber

01:47:38.260 --> 01:47:41.560
in your diet might be
useful for increasing

01:47:41.560 --> 01:47:44.030
fiber digesting enzymes
and the assimilation

01:47:44.030 --> 01:47:45.280
of fibrous foods

01:47:45.280 --> 01:47:48.030
but that it's really the
ingestion of fermented foods

01:47:48.030 --> 01:47:50.070
and, in fact, getting anywhere from four

01:47:50.070 --> 01:47:52.450
or even up to six servings
a day of fermented foods

01:47:52.450 --> 01:47:55.100
can be immensely beneficial for reducing

01:47:55.100 --> 01:47:56.710
inflammatory markers in the body

01:47:56.710 --> 01:47:59.870
and for improving microbiota diversity

01:47:59.870 --> 01:48:03.750
all along the gut and
thereby improving signaling

01:48:03.750 --> 01:48:07.660
and outcomes along the gut-brain axis.

01:48:07.660 --> 01:48:11.060
So we went all the way
from structure to function

01:48:11.060 --> 01:48:12.530
to the four kinds of signaling,

01:48:12.530 --> 01:48:14.780
mechanical, chemical, indirect, direct,

01:48:14.780 --> 01:48:17.730
probiotics, fiber, and fermented foods.

01:48:17.730 --> 01:48:20.320
And I tossed in a little
bit at the end there also

01:48:20.320 --> 01:48:23.450
about ways that you can make
your own fermented foods

01:48:23.450 --> 01:48:25.720
at home in order to try and
offset some of the costs.

01:48:25.720 --> 01:48:27.350
Also, it's just kind of fun to do.

01:48:27.350 --> 01:48:29.590
And some of those
actually taste quite good.

01:48:29.590 --> 01:48:32.590
I've actually found that
